\par\par \b \uc2  Yen Cross - Mark steady on investor buying demand. \b0\par\par\uc2 472 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 30 (Reuters) - The German mark was mostly steady against the yen in becalmed cross trade on Thursday, with Japanese investors keen to pick up marks on falls, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Although trade was subdued in amidst Japan's Golden Week holiday, investors, including trust banks, felt comfortable placing bids for marks, said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  "This is largely because of the absence of fears of any BOJ (Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the mark/yen arena," he added. The mark was at 73.64/67 yen at 0504 GMT against 73.64 yen in late Wednesday trade in New York. \par \par \uc2  "The mark is on an upward path, we could probably see the mark climb to 74 yen in May and head towards 75 or 76 yen," said the dealer at the European bank. \par \par \uc2  He said there is a resistance point at 74.10/15 yen but once the mark clearly breaks through that point, there would be no major obstacle on the charts. \par \par \uc2  A Japanese bank dealer said Japanese exporters are keen to sell marks above 74 yen, which has partly prevented the mark from rising against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Many traders here expect the German Bundesbank Bank not to raise interest rates at its regular council meeting later in the day ahead of the EU summit on May 1-3. \par \par \uc2  But lingering expectations of higher German interest rates could contribute to the strength of the mark, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The mark strengthened in early New York on Wednesday after comments from Bundesbank President Hans Tietmeyer were first interpreted to mean Germany could raise rates. Speaking in Leipzig, Tietmeyer said the central monetary union rate would depend on a mix of national policies. \par \par \uc2  The mark then retreated after Bundesbank council member Klaus-Dieter Kuehbacher was quoted in Germany's Berliner Zeitung newspaper as saying he saw no sensible reason to raise rates in 1998. \par \par \uc2  "Judging from a slew of recent remarks by various Bundesbank officials, a German rate increase is not imminent," said a senior dealer at another Eurpean bank. \par \par \uc2  "But rate hike could be possible sometime before the start of a single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an attempt to converge its rate with others," he added. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the EU summit was unlikely to cause the financial market any fresh confusion over the outlook for the "euro", which is likely to make a smooth start in 1999. \par \par \uc2  The only focus concerning the EU summit would be on the dispute over the presidency of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  Tietmeyer said on Wednesday European Union leaders must resolve the dispute over the presidency of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  without breaking the Maastricht Treaty's rules. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk 81-3-3432-1396, email:tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4u06b4a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
