\par\par \b \uc2  Bleak Japan data weighs on Tokyo stocks and yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kanta Watanabe \par \uc2 838 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 1998\par 07:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 28 (Reuters) - Tokyo stocks fell on Tuesday after a sharp overnight sell-off on Wall Street and a set of Japanese economic data reminding the market that any turnaround in the Japanese economy was unlikely in the near term. \par \par \uc2  The bleak March data - a surge in the jobless rate to a record 3.9 percent, a fall in industrial output of a preliminary 1.9 percent and a double-digit decline in retail sales - also helped to push the dollar higher against the yen in early trade in the Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The key 225-share Nikkei average fell as low as 15,334.47, down more than two percent, before ending the day down 254.52 points or 1.63 percent at 15,395.43. \par \par \uc2  "Investors' sentiment has been bearish since the market was disappointed at the lack of permanent tax cuts in the government's package," said Kunihiro Hatae, a general manager at Tokyo Securities Co Ltd. \par \par \uc2  Hatae was referring to a stimulus package worth more than 16 trillion yen ($121 billion) announced by the Japanese government last Friday. \par \par \uc2  The package contained 12 trillion in fresh fiscal spending, but lacked the permanent tax cuts that markets were seeking. \par \par \uc2  The bearish mood was exacerbated by an overnight tumble in U.S. stock and bond prices on concerns that the U.S. Federal Reserve would raise interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average ended down 146.98 points or 1.62 percent at 8,917.64 on Monday, while the yield of the 30-year U.S. Treasury bond rose to 6.05 percent on Monday from 5.95 percent last Friday. \par \par \uc2  Speculation of Fed tightening had mounted on Monday after the Wall Street Journal reported that Fed policy-makers adopted a tightening bias in the March meeting of the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC). \par \par \uc2  But such speculation eased by Tuesday afternoon in Tokyo, following a Washington Post report quoting Fed sources as saying U.S. rate increase may not come soon. \par \par \uc2  A decision in the March meeting to "lean" towards higher short-term rates did not necessarily mean higher rates were on the way soon, the paper quoted unnamed Fed sources as saying. \par \par \uc2  The dollar initially slipped on this report, but quickly pared losses on expectations that Wall Street could show a solid rebound later on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But lingering concerns about possible dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) helped to cap the dollar's upside, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar earlier rose as high as 132.64 yen before heavy sales emerged to knock it down to the day's lows of 131.35 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Some players initially believed the dollar's fall was caused by BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but many dealers said later that the sales were done by overseas fund operators and Japanese life insurers, not the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said comments by visiting U.S. Secretary of State Madeleine Albright were also supportive for the yen. \par \par \uc2  Asked how the United States viewed the measures announced by Japan on Friday, Albright told a news conference: "Generally, we see this is as a bold and important step forward by the (Japanese) government." \par \par \uc2  "Albright's comments suggested that the U.S. was content for now with Japan's actions on the economy. That should take some downward pressure off the yen," said a manager of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk at a long-term credit bank. \par \par \uc2  The dollar eased slightly around midday after Japan's Foreign Minister Keizo Obuchi said Japan's trade imbalance with the United States was due to yen's weakness against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Japan must stimulate its economy to boost imports so as not to have an export drive, and carefully act on the yen/dollar rate to solve the trade imbalance between Japan and the United States," Obuchi said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 131.90/00 yen in late Tokyo, compared with 132.10/15 yen in New York on late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Japanese government bonds (JGBs) surged to record levels - with some market participants seeing new highs in the near future - after a successful auction of six-year notes and amid fading concern over a rate rise in the United States. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the benchmark 182nd government bond fell as low as 1.455 percent, while the key June futures contract surged to 131.39. \par \par \uc2  "It's like we can no longer see the ceiling," said a trader at a major Japanese financial institution. "After the auction results, we just kept going." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new 1.4 percent coupon note sold for a low price of 100.13 yen per 100 yen in face value. \par \par \uc2  But bond traders said demand for the bond was so strong it was being quoted at 100.20 on the secondary market after the results were released. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 9780 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4s06ipq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
