\par\par \b \uc2  No wind of change seen for yen after MoF comments. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Astrid Zweynert \par \uc2 863 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 1998\par 12:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 27 (Reuters) - The yen will remain mired despite warnings by senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MoF) official Eisuke Sakakibara that there is a risk on the upside for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The yen fell to four-month lows against the mark, close to 74 yen, and fell further against the dollar to reach 2-1/2 week lows near 133. \par \par \uc2  Traders are likely to push the yen even lower in a data-packed week in the United States and Japan which is expected to emphasise the gaping difference between the two economies. \par \par \uc2  "We should be looking at 135 soon on dollar/yen because the market's view on Japan's economic fundamentals is still bearish, and Sakakibara's comments have failed to change that," said Nick Probert, head of spot trading at Bank of America in London. \par \par \uc2  At 1030 GMT the dollar was at 132.57/67 yen, up 1.2 percent from late European levels on Friday. Dollar/mark was sidelined, trading at 1.7952/62 from 1.7916/21 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, vice finance minister for international affairs at the MoF, said in an interview with Reuters Television on Monday that bullish sentiment for the dollar was starting to change and that Tokyo was looking for the right time to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  "with the wind" to boost the yen. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese economy was expected to turn around in six to nine months, Sakakibara reiterated, stressing that markets usually turn around faster than the real economy. \par \par \uc2  "The Sakakibara comments failed to instil confidence in the yen," said Claudio Piron, treasury economist at Standard Chartered in London. \par \par \uc2  "The market is not convinced that the Japanese government is willing to implement the necessary fiscal measures for a stronger yen." \par \par \uc2  Yen bears made a further stab at the downside on Monday after Kazuo Ueda, a member of the Bank of Japan's policy-making board, said on Monday a further depreciation of five to 10 percent in the yen's value against the dollar would not pose a very large economic risk to Japan. \par \par \uc2  His comments fanned speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not yet worried by the yen's fall. \par \par \uc2  Ueda also said he would like to see a cut in Japan's official discount rate, already at a record low of 0.5 percent, if economic conditions worsened. His view on interest rates was seen as a minority stance. \par \par \uc2  While Ueda's comments fanned pessimism about the yen's prospects, they were not enough to overshadow completely the fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The MoF is trying to talk the yen stronger but there is no secret level they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at and their policy is only to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they are going to be successful," said Gerard Lyons, chief economist at DKB International in London. \par \par \uc2  The key to successful \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lies in dispelling gloom about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic outlook, which has persisted despite the adoption of an economic stimulus package worth 16 trillion yen ($121.1 billion), details of which were announced on Friday, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The measures were much as expected, though fell short of expectations that at least some of the four trillion yen in income tax cuts included in the package would be permanent. \par \par \uc2  Data out overnight did little to lift pessimism about Japanese domestic demand, with trade statistics for the first 10 days of April showing Japan's surplus soared 113.8 percent from a year earlier, with imports slumping 13.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  The dire state of the economy is also expected to be reflected in March industrial production, retail sales and unemployment data due out of Japan this week, which should confirm the market's view that domestic interest rates will not be raised in the foreseeable future. \par \par \uc2  The prospect of higher U.S. rates - and scope for dollar gains - will grab the market's attention later this week when the U.S. first-quarter employment cost index and preliminary gross domestic product data is released on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The employment cost index, closely watched by the Fed's Open Market Committee as a harbinger for wage inflation, is expected to show a rise of 0.9 percent after 1.0 percent in the last quarter. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the April National Association of Purchasing Management's survey is due and will give the market clues about the strength of the manufacturing industry. \par \par \uc2  "It's quite a classic combination for the dollar to go higher, and a stronger dollar should put additional pressure on the yen," said Piron at Standard Chartered. \par \par \uc2  The Wall Street Journal said on Monday the Fed was leaning towards raising interest rates because the economy was not slowing as much as it expected in the wake of Asia's economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  Last week, Fed Governor Roger Ferguson said rates will be raised to prevent the economy from overheating unless Asia's crisis dampens the frenzied pace of growth soon. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the only data release during the European session will be U.S. March existing home sales at 1400 GMT. ($ = 132.1 Japanese Yen). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4r06s65\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\pi