\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's Mr Yen - no stranger to controversy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 741 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 1998\par 12:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 27 (Reuters) - Japan's top financial diplomat, Eisuke Sakakibara, was back in the news on Monday - but not merely for moving \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  The controversial Sakakibara, long known as "Mr Yen" for his ability to affect exchange rates with a single comment, was among the 112 \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) officials punished on Monday for accepting lavish entertainment from private firms. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga announced the shock action against Sakakibara and other officials in a statement at the end of an internal probe of more than 1,050 MOF staff who had contacts with the private sector over the past five years. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, now vice finance minister for international affairs, has for the past three years been a rarity in Japan - a nail that sticks up but was not hammered down. \par \par \uc2  But a controversy which swirled around him earlier this year brought into sharp focus two dueling aspects of his role: his unique ability to articulate Japan's economic policy and a tendency to ruffle feathers at home with his stand-alone ways. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara came under fire in March for alleged influence peddling when an opposition party member told a key parliamentary panel that he had learned that in 1991 Sakakibara helped an acquaintance who was a company president to obtain compensation of around 220 million yen ($1.65 million) from Daiwa Securities for losses suffered in trading warrants. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said then that there was no evidence to back up the allegations and that he did not think the reopening of a Finance Ministry investigation into his conduct would have any impact on his career. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara had been in hot water earlier after being hauled before the Lower House budget committee and dressed down by another opposition party member for having agreed at a Group of Seven (G7) meeting on a statement citing a view that Japan needed to do more to stimulate its stagnant economy. \par \par \uc2  He also drew fire for criticising certain aspects of the parliamentary system and for suggesting that Japan's U.S.-drafted post-war Constitution needed revising. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara has long been dogged by controversy at home, where his aggressive personality created enemies, and abroad, where he won a reputation for his tough stance in financial negotiations with Washington. \par \par \uc2  But like him or not, Sakakibara has often been the main - if not sole - articulator abroad of Japan's often perplexing policies. \par \par \uc2  He has also been credited with spearheading a drive to counter the yen's steep rise in 1995, with pushing Tokyo toward "Big Bang" financial reforms that kicked of on April 1, and with urging Japanese authorities to play a more prominent role in Asia. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, however, there were some signs that Sakakibara's seemingly magical ability to nudge the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the direction he wants it to go might be fading a bit. \par \par \uc2  Speaking to Reuters Television in an interview arranged at his own request to "clarify" comments he made in an interview late on Friday, Sakakibara said he believed that a mammoth Japanese stimulus package would boost the economy within six to nine months and that the battered yen might well turn around before that. \par \par \uc2  Citing a long spell of dollar bullishness in the market, Sakakibara said, "I feel that the wind is beginning to change or is changing", adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  timed as the wind was changing could be "quite effective". \par \par \uc2  In Friday's interview, Sakakibara had said the dollar-yen trend would turn around "somewhere" in the next six to nine months. That, combined with his comment that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy was to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market "with the wind" encouraged some market players to think chances had lessened for Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the weak yen. \par \par \uc2  News that Sakakibara would speak again on Monday morning pushed the dollar below 131 yen in early Tokyo trade. \par \par \uc2  But the greenback climbed back after he spoke and - aided by comments from Bank of Japan policy board member Kazuo Ueda - marched to 132.40 yen in the late afternoon. The dollar was trading at about 132.63 yen in early evening in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "Each time Mr Sakakibara speaks the dollar goes up now, which was unlikely in the past," said a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a big Japanese bank. ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4r06gdn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
