\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr steady at midday Europe ahead of Japan package. \b0\par\par\uc2 768 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 24 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar struggled off earlier lows against the yen by midday in Europe, but remained tentatively softer from Thursday's levels before official details of Japan's latest economic stimulus package. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the dollar remained capped below 130 yen on news of a draft version of the plan, which showed 12 trillion yen worth of new stimulus. \par \par \uc2  "The numbers are slightly bigger than people had thought and the Nikkei is up nearly 250 points, and that's the reason the dollar remains below 130," said Michael Lewis senior international economist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell. \par \par \uc2  At 0935 GMT, dollar/yen struggled up to 129.70/80 yen from Asian lows around 129.45, but was still down from its 130.32/33 level in late European trading on Thursday. Dollar/mark was trading at 1.7965/70, off a session low of 1.7925, but down from Thursday's 1.8005/10. \par \par \uc2  Japan's benchmark Nikkei index closed up 249.55 points, or 1.58 percent, on hints of future permanent income tax cuts. \par \par \uc2  "The LDP's (Liberal Democratic Party) tax panel, I believe, said in its final proposal for tax cuts that it will consider permanent income tax cuts, including permanent cuts in corporate taxes," said Japan's Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga. \par \par \uc2  However, later reflection of Matsunaga's comments and the absence of fresh clues about permanent tax cuts in the reported draft of Japan's stimulus plan underpinned the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there was concern the Bank of Japan was stalking the foreign exchanges with a double-edged sword after a top financial bureaucrat spooked the market with talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "I could see the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  coming in if the dollar goes up or the dollar goes down and if that's the case, do I really want to be buying dollars here," said Cameron Crise, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at SBC Warburg in London. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs, Eisuke Sakakibara, said on Thursday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not defend specific levels, but that it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it thought the yen would rise. \par \par \uc2  A range of other news events also swirled around the dollar as speculation continued to grow about the likelihood of a German rate rise this year, and dollar/Swiss franc wobbled ahead of a vote in the Russian Duma. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark was off its lows despite comments from two Bundesbank council members, who joined their voices to the chorus attempting to dampen speculation over a German rate rise. \par \par \uc2  "Either they are telling us a story and they are going to jack up rates very soon, which is our view, or they are still thinking only about domestic issues," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank council member Franz-Christoph Zeitler said on Friday that Germany's interest rate level was appropriate from a domestic viewpoint. His comments were echoed by Bundesbank council member Helmut Hesse who said the bank could not move interest rates without consulting its European economic and monetary union (EMU) partners. Hesse said it was unclear at present if the next German rate move would be up or down. \par \par \uc2  "But central banks say that monetary policy is formulated with a longer-term view and the Bundesbank's policy has to be seen taking a pan-European view if it still wants to see itself as forerunner of the ECB (European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," Barrow said. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc slid against the dollar and the mark after the chances improved that Russian President Boris Yeltsin's candidate for prime minister, Sergei Kiriyenko, would be ratified by the lower house of parliament. \par \par \uc2  The mark rose against the Swiss franc to 83.04/08 centimes from a session low of 82.67 centimes and the dollar jumped to 1.4922/43 Swiss francs from a session low of 1.4840. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the Swiss franc's safe-haven status was eroded on news the Duma would vote on Kiriyenko using a secret ballot. Reuters reported that some Russian Communist party members were taking ballot slips for the vote in spite of a party pledge not to participate in the secret vote. \par \par \uc2  This is expected to increase Kiriyenko's chances of endorsement by the Communist-dominated parliament and stave off the prospect of new elections. President Yeltsin has vowed to dissolve parliament if parliament rejects Kiriyenko. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4o06sgy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
