\par\par \b \uc2  Yen gets little support from package, Sakakibara. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Pratima Desai \par \uc2 694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 1998\par 17:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 24 (Reuters) - Attempts by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to talk up the fiscal stimulus announced on Friday did little to dispel negative yen sentiment, and analysts said they expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slowly drift south against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Japan's vice finance minister, Eisuke Sakakibara, said the fresh 12 trillion yen fiscal stimulus announced earlier on Friday would boost gross domestic product for fiscal year 1998 by two percent to three percentage points. \par \par \uc2  "He's trying to talk up the announcement, but dollar/yen is still trading around the 130 level," said Keith Edmonds, chief analyst at IBJ International, immediately after Sakakibara's comments. \par \par \uc2  "Once the Golden Week holiday is over I expect to see the dollar moving back towards 135," he said, referring to a string of Japanese national holidays which begins on April 29 and ends May 5. \par \par \uc2  For now though, analysts said fear of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen was holding back dollar bulls. \par \par \uc2  At 1430 GMT the dollar was trading at 130.88/98 yen, little changed from 130.32/33 late Thursday. This was about 3.5 percent below the six-and-half-year high of 135.42 yen seen earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  Nick Shamim, bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ANZ Investment bank, also expects to see a higher dollar/yen. He said dollar/yen is good value at 127.00 yen and over the next three months expects it to drift up to 135.00. \par \par \uc2  "Everything the government has tried has failed to ignite economic growth... the conclusion is that new spending has had no impact on growth," he said. \par \par \uc2  A slow drift downwards rather than a sharp drop in yen levels may be more acceptable to the United States and Japan, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The bottom line is that Japan's economy is suffering from deflation and the spiral is worsening, this will weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Shamim said, adding that he sees dollar/yen at 138.00 in six months' time. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also said Sakakibara's comments to the effect that the government's tax panel was discussing an adjustment of tax rates, not permanent cuts as hoped for by the markets, would also weigh the yen down. \par \par \uc2  The income tax cuts totalling four trillion yen which Japan has already announced would not be a real boost for the economy, they added. \par \par \uc2  "Tax cuts will have little effect on consumer confidence. Japan has such a huge propensity to save," Shamim said. "They have two choices left. They will have to cut interest rates maybe to negative levels and/or they can print money." \par \par \uc2  Another problem highlighted by analysts is the bad debt problem in Japan's financial sector. \par \par \uc2  "Ultimately my criticism is that none of these measures address the bad debt problem in the banking sector," said Tim Harris, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at National Australia Bank. \par \par \uc2  "The package today is not enough in itself to strengthen the yen. From a fundamental perspective the yen should be weaker." \par \par \uc2  Over the next three to six months, Harris sees dollar/yen moving towards the 140-150 level. \par \par \uc2  The catalyst for the yen's drop could come from external factors such as growing expectations of higher U.S. interest rates or a worsening in the Asian financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  "There is a growing groundswell of opinion that the Federal Reserve is going to raise interest rates. I don't think they will but I accept the market is beginning to believe this," Harris said. \par \par \uc2  The prospect of wage inflation in the United States from tight labour markets and skills shortages has led many analysts to expect a rise in the Federal funds rate, currently at 5.5 percent. By contrast Japan's discount rate, the lowest in the world, is at 0.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  A further deterioration in the Asian crisis would be another shock for the Japanese economy and its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The true effects from Asia will continue maybe for another two or three months, and what if China were to devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Where would that leave the yen?" Harris said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4o06qjb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
