\par\par \b \uc2  WEEK AHEAD - European markets eye EU summit, US data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Clelia Oziel \par \uc2 776 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 24 (Reuters) - European financial markets will concentrate on the decision on membership of the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the coming week, ahead of the European Union's crucial weekend summit, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. data will also provided some focus and analysts said on Friday that they would also be monitoring further developments in Japan, and the dollar/yen, after the government's mammoth economic package that included more than 12 trillion yen real stimulus. \par \par \uc2  But given no permanent cuts in income tax and corporate taxes were announced, analysts said the dollar could spike higher again, re-kindling speculation about Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In the U.S., the highlight of the week will be the all-important employment cost index (ECI) for the first quarter which economists will scrutinise to decide which direction U.S. interest rates are heading. \par \par \uc2  In Europe, with most of the decisions regarding economic and monetary union (EMU) already made, the May 1-3 meeting to select the founding members formally may produce little for the markets to chew on. \par \par \uc2  "It really is a grand photo session," said David Brickman, international economist at PaineWebber in London. \par \par \uc2  But the only remaining issue to be resolved, the decision on who heads the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB), may rattle the markets if European leaders fail to find a compromise between the Dutch candidate, Wim Duisenberg and France's Jean-Claude Trichet. \par \par \uc2  Most European markets will be closed on Friday for Labour Day. Britain has a Bank holiday on Monday but the London International Financial Futures and Options Exchange (LIFFE) will open for trade. \par \par \uc2  Robert Hayward, international economist at BankAmerica in London, said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets could come under continued speculation over a possible revaluation of the Irish punt and Dutch guilder next week before bilateral exchange rates are fixed at the weekend. \par \par \uc2  And continued deadlock over the ECB head might cause some underperformance of European markets, he said, but added: "I think most it's likely they'll come up with something at the last minute." \par \par \uc2  PaineWebber's Brickman said the Dutch were unlikely to give ground on the issue before the country's general election on May 6. \par \par \uc2  "Once they are out of the way, then the road is open for a compromise," he said. \par \par \uc2  On the election front, Germany will grab the headlines with its eastern state of Saxony-Anhalt, where Social Democrat leader Gerhard Schroeder SPD party is tipped to win a majority of the vote. \par \par \uc2  Such an outcome could further threaten Chancellor Helmut Kohl's chances of staying in power after the general election in September. \par \par \uc2  The Bundesbank holds its regular council meeting on Thursday but economists do not expect a rise in interest rates. \par \par \uc2  U.S. data will kick off with March durable goods and April consumer confidence on Tuesday. But Thursday's ECI will be more important. The market consensus is a rise of 0.9 percent, after a 1.0 percent rise previously. \par \par \uc2  "The latest judgement on the extent to which tight labour markets are feeding wages growth will be largely determined by the outcome of Thursday's ECI," said Nomura in a research note to clients. \par \par \uc2  PaineWebber's Brickman said: "If it comes in above 0.9 the market will be a little concerned about the possible talk about Fed tightening. Conversely, if it comes below it, the market will be relieved," he said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. first quarter GDP is due on Thursday, but analysts said this might be eclipsed by the ECI data. NAPM for April will be released on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "On the whole, we expect that financial markets will begin to sense that slower growth is ahead," said Nomura. \par \par \uc2  In the UK, analysts said there might be further evidence of the manufacturing sector suffering under the burden of sterling strength. \par \par \uc2  This could suggest the Bank of England will hold interest rates steady at its May policy meeting. \par \par \uc2  February and March trade data are out on Tuesday and CBI industrial trends survey for April on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A slew of Japanese data will be analysed for any evidence that the economy is plunging into a recession and deflation. The main item will be preliminary March industrial production on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "The risk there is that we could see dollar/yen make a tentative move to the upside," said Hayward. \par \par \uc2  Other data due in the course of the week include French unemployment, UK consumer credit, U.S. personal income and personal spending. ((International Bonds +44 171 542 8663 fax +44 171 542 5285, uk.governmentbonds.news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4o06qhv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
