\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar/yen shrugs off Japan package. \b0\par\par\uc2 754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 24 (Reuters) - The dollar ended a hectic session firmer against the yen on Friday despite Japan's approval of a 16 trillion yen economic package. \par \par \uc2  The yen responded cautiously to the package and then lost ground against the dollar and mark in late European trade after Eisuke Sakakibara, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, provided little to thwart yen bears in his news briefing following announcement of the package. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers also contended with the Russian Duma's approval of Sergei Kiriyenko as prime minister, which weighed on the safe-haven Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  "The package was a bit larger than expected, but Sakakibara didn't offer anything really new in his comments," said a U.S. bank dealer. "The market was looking for something definite on the question of permanent income tax cuts, but it looks like it's going to have to wait. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen climbed as high as 131.20 in afternoon trade, up from levels around 129.60-70 before the package's approval. By 1455 GMT it stood at 130.84/94 yen. \par \par \uc2  Mark/yen also pushed higher, rising from earlier levels around 72.20-30 to 72.90/95 in late trade. It stood at 72.38/43 late Thursday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Japan's economic package to stimulate its domestic economy was much as expected though fell short of expectations that at least some of the four-trillion yen in income tax cuts included in the stimulus measures would be made permanent. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara's comments that the government tax panel was discussing an adjustment in tax rates dimmed the prospect of future, permanent cuts. And analysts were sceptical that tax cuts alone would be enough to reflate the economy. \par \par \uc2  "Tax cuts will have little effect on consumer confidence. Japan has such a huge propensity to save," said Nick Shamim, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ANZ Investment Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  However, dollar/yen's move higher was relatively tentative and dealers said volume was light. Wariness of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop the yen still remains despite comments from Sakakibara on Friday and Thursday saying that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action would not be defensive. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Swiss franc was weaker against the mark and dollar as Russia's lower house of parliament, the Duma, approved Kiriyenko as prime minister in a secret ballot. \par \par \uc2  The vote averted the prospect of new elections in Russia and took the shine off the safe-have Swiss franc which had firmed this week amid Russia's political uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  "The market has been watching the situation which has been part of the reason for recent movements in the Swiss franc," said Jeremy Stretch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at NatWest Markets in London. "We've seen a bit of a move this morning and we'll probably see it go a bit lower." \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar the Swiss franc stood at 1.4930/40 per dollar, off an earlier low of 1.4960 and against 1.4910/20 late Thursday. Mark/Swiss was trading at 83.27/30 centimes from 82.81/82 centimes Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark, meantime, stuck to a broad 1.79-1.80 range for the European session. \par \par \uc2  Comments from Bundesbank members Helmut Hesse and Franz-Christoph Zeitler had little impact on dollar/mark. \par \par \uc2  Hesse said it was unclear if the next move in German interest rates would be up or down and changes would not be made without consulting Germany's EMU partners first. \par \par \uc2  Both members were sanguine about \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels with Zeitler saying he did not see price pressures coming from current \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. Zeitler also said German interest rates were appropriate, echoing similar Bundesbank members' comments this week. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark was trading at 1.7901/11 in late trade, testing session lows and below late Thursday's 1.8005/10 marks. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was another main mover on the day, trading at session lows against the mark after earlier trying to breach upside resistance at 3.00 marks. \par \par \uc2  Dealers put its late retreat down to dollar/mark fluctuations but also said today's slightly lower than forecast UK GDP data added to this week's round of economic data indicating UK interest rates may have peaked. \par \par \uc2  Sterling/mark stood at 2.9870/80 in late trade, down from 2.9978/88 late Thursday. Cable was trading at $1.6652/62 against $1.6650/60. \par \par \uc2  ((Andrew Reierson, London newsroom +44 171 542 6745, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4o06ppx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
