\par\par \b \uc2  Firm dollar/yen holds fire at European midsession. \b0\par\par\uc2 708 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 23 (Reuters) - The dollar was firm against the yen at European midsession on Thursday as the market waited for details of Japan's stimulus package due on Friday, which most believe will do little to boost the yen. \par \par \uc2  An early dollar boost on the back of comments by Eisuke Sakakibara, Japan's vice finance minister for International Affairs, stalled ahead of 131 yen as dealers stayed sidelined ahead of the release of the package details. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said lingering concern that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) will accompany the release with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  capped the dollar's rise. There was also talk of offers around 131 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The spotlight is firmly on Japan and the release of the package, so the market has stalled for now," said Mike Wallace, manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysis at Standard & Poor's MMS. \par \par \uc2  "But the dollar is firmly underpinned by expectations the yen will weaken if the package just meets market expectations." \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen peaked after Sakakibara said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it believes the yen will rise. It will not conduct defensive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain a specific level, he said. \par \par \uc2  At 1130 GMT the dollar was at 130.41/51 yen, off an earlier high at 130.77 and against 130.80/90 in late Europe Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The reaction to Sakakibara's comments underlined the market's sensitivity to any talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Wallace said concern about BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was reflected in the options market where dealers noted demand for very short-dated yen calls with strikes around 128 to protect against BOJ action on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively and pushed dollar/yen down sharply on April 9 and 10 after the market failed to react to earlier details of this economic package. \par \par \uc2  The measures include a 16 trillion yen economic stimulus package and steps to stabilise the financial system. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto has promised the details of the package will include at least 10 trillion yen in new fiscal stimulus, with four trillion yen in income tax cuts over the next two years. \par \par \uc2  "It remains questionable how much of the tax cuts will be actually spent and how effective BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be over the medium-term," said Michael Lewis, international economist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen bulls were restrained though by further comments from Sakakibara, who said Japan's economy and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate were at a significant turning point. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark hovered above 1.79, with little fresh impetus for the market's views on German interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rebounded from a near three-month at 1.7815 hit on Thursday after several German central bankers poured cold water on speculation that domestic rates would rise soon by saying there was no reason for such a move. \par \par \uc2  But talk that a European \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had offers lined up between 1.7950 and 1.80 marks capped the dollar, while the downside was well protected by talk of good bids by an Asian player in in the upper 1.78 area, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Sterling/mark's continued weakness added to the dollar's difficulties on the upside as the pound fell to a seven-week low below 2.99 marks amid receding interest rate rise speculation. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc was a touch stronger against the dollar and the mark. Dollar/Swiss traded at 1.4830/40 from 1.4854/64 late on Thursday, while mark/Swiss stood at 82.75/80 from 82.82/85. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was watching the political situation in Russia ahead of Friday's vote when parliament must vote for prime-minister designate Sergei Kiriyenko or face dissolution. \par \par \uc2  The Communist and nationalist dominated Duma has twice rejected Kiriyenko. Communist leader Gennady Zyuganov said on Thursday the party's ruling body had decided to advise a wider leadership meeting to stick to its opposition to the Kremlin's candidate for prime minister. \par \par \uc2  A "no" vote would raise the spectre of increased political uncertainty in Russia and could prompt safe-haven flows into the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  ((Astrid Zweynert, London newsroom, +44 171 542 7792, fax +44 542 52 93, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011004du4n00jgu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
