\par\par \b \uc2  Japan ministry under fire over G7  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  statement. \b0\par\par\uc2  By George Nishiyama \par \uc2 645 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 1998\par 14:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 23 (Reuters) - Amid growing signs of its irritation with \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  came under fire on Thursday for playing up a G7 statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen. \par \par \uc2  An opposition member of parliament accused the ministry of deliberately mistranslating the latest Group of Seven statement to suggest support for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and play down the group's real message that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can only boost the yen by stimulating domestic demand and reducing its external surpluses. \par \par \uc2  "The ministry has mistranslated the statement. The (G7) nations gave support to Japan's measures to stimulate domestic demand and efforts to reduce the external imbalance but do not necessary give clear support regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Yoshio Suzuki of the opposition Liberal Party told a parliamentary committee. \par \par \uc2  A senior Finance Ministry official rejected Suzuki's view, saying the G7 statement indicated the possibility of joint action on exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  The statement "discusses the possibility of cooperative action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market", said Haruhiko Kuroda, head of the ministry's International Finance Bureau. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda was referring to the part of the statement which said: "We will continue to monitor developments in exchange markets and to cooperate as appropriate." \par \par \uc2  But the statement also said: "We support appropriate steps by Japan aimed at stimulating domestic demand-led growth and reducing external imbalances, thus also correcting the excessive depreciation of the yen." \par \par \uc2  The dispute highlighted attempts by Japanese authorities to talk up the yen and also the apparent discord among key G7 nations over \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  Just as Kuroda was speaking, another prominent figure was warning the markets that the recent trend of a strong dollar and a weak yen was at a "turning point". \par \par \uc2  "Both the Japanese economy and the exchange rate are at a significant turning point," Eisuke Sakakibara, the Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs told a symposium. \par \par \uc2  He also said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when it believed the yen would rise as a result, but added: "We do not have in mind at all defensive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain a specific level." \par \par \uc2  The comments were interpreted by the market as a sign of Tokyo's reluctance to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the yen and had the effect of pushing up the dollar. \par \par \uc2  After Sakakibara spoke, the dollar rose to around 130.80 yen, the intraday high, from a New York close of 130.35/40. \par \par \uc2  The resilience of the dollar has irritated Japanese officials and even led them to put blame on other nations. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week, Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga criticised his U.S. counterpart, Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, for lessening the impact of a yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early April. \par \par \uc2  "Immediately after we took steps to correct the excessive yen weakness, Secretary Rubin made a statement to support Japan's action. But later on a different occasion, Rubin said a strong dollar is in the U.S.'s interest. As a result (his remark) weakened the effect of our action to correct the yen's excessive weakness," Matsunaga said. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday in a rare move, Sakakibara touched on the U.S. economy, saying it had "peaked out" and the U.S. stock prices would also peak out. \par \par \uc2  Market participants said the comments reflected the frustration of Japanese officials with the attitude of their U.S. counterparts. \par \par \uc2  "We all know that deep in their hearts, the U.S. wants a strong dollar," said Yasuhito Kawashima, chief manager at the trading department of Toyo Trust & Banking Co. \par \par \uc2  "Washington is taking a common stand on 'sharing concern over a weak yen' with Tokyo, but very reluctantly." ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4n06u21\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
