\par\par \b \uc2  OPTIONS - Dlr/mark vols slip back as spot rebounds. \b0\par\par\uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 22 (Reuters) - Implied volatilities for dollar/mark options eased from early European peaks after comments by a German central banker lifted spot from near-three-month lows. \par \par \uc2  Vols spiked higher early in the session as spot dollar/mark fell as low as 1.7820, its weakest since January 28, in a continuation of the downmove triggered by Tuesday's break below 1.7950 technical support. \par \par \uc2  Spot clawed back ground, however, after Bundesbank council member Klaus-Dieter Kuehbacher said on Wednesday he saw no need to raise German interest rates and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had no interest in allowing the dollar to slide against the mark. \par \par \uc2  "Vols got pushed higher as spot set new lows early on, but all the bids disappeared after Kuehbacher's comments about rates saw dollar/mark go back up," said a trader at a continental European bank in London. \par \par \uc2  To be sure, "people are still afraid of the downside and like short mark calls," he said. \par \par \uc2  Implied volatility for one-month dollar/mark options was at 9.60/9.80 percent at 1030 GMT, from 9.80/10.10 percent on Tuesday. Meantime, one-year implied vol dipped to 9.70/9.90 percent from 9.80/10.10 percent late yesterday. \par \par \uc2  Risk reversals for one-month 25-delta options showed a skew of 0.35/0.65 towards mark calls against the dollar, compared with Tuesday's early peaks of 0.85 and late European closing bias of 0.4/0.6. \par \par \uc2  Mark/yen vols also eased as spot shied away from mounting a serious challenge of the 74.15 technical barrier. \par \par \uc2  One-month vol was at 12.80/13.10 percent from 13.00/13.30 percent at the close of European trading on Tuesday while one-year vol fell to 12.80/13.00 percent from 13.15/13.55 percent late yesterday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Risk reversals are around par compared with a slight bias towards mark calls against the yen late in Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Spot mark/yen was undermined as much by mark weakness as by gains the yen made amid rising wariness of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  Such concern was triggered after Eisuke Sakakibara, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's vice finance minister for international affairs, said on Wednesday investors should consider the risks of a higher yen and said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had ample reserves to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. ((Swaha Pattanaik, London newsroom, +44 171 542 6284, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4m070b3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
