\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar falls on yen after Japan says it may sell bonds. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alden Bentley \par \uc2 451 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 1998\par 22:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, April 22 (Reuters) - The dollar fell against the yen on Wednesday after Japan indicated it might cash in some of its vast U.S. investments to boost its beleagured \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the dollar rose against the German mark, bouncing from a three-month low set overnight, after several German central bankers dashed speculation that German interest rates would soon rise. \par \par \uc2  Pushing the dollar down was a remark from influential \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Eisuke Sakakibara that Japan stood ready to convert U.S. Treasury bonds into dollars to fund \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  His comment at a Tokyo symposium reflected irritation that the yen remained weak after two big rounds of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before Easter and a statement by Group of Seven finance officials the following week welcoming Japanese steps to boost its economy and strengthen the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The Treasury (bond) comments were very much out of character," said Ben Strauss, a vice president at Bank Julius Baer and Co. "It could be that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is around the corner again." \par \par \uc2  After \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent market action, talk was widespread that Japan had sold about $12 billion in Treasury securities to raise the cash. \par \par \uc2  Due to its huge trade surplus, Japan has amassed the world's largest war chest of dollar reserves, estimated at more the $200 billion. It is the largest foreign holder of U.S. government bonds, with some 25 percent of Washington's debt. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Japan is expected to detail a 16 trillion yen ($123 billion) package of measures to revive its faltering economy. Investors speculated \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may time any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the announcement, as it has done with previous economic packages. A Cabinet vote on the plan may come on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 130.43 yen, down from 131.25 at Tuesday's close and at 1.7940 marks, up from 1.7884. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose against the mark after Bundesbank council members Klaus-Dieter Kuehbacher, Reimut Jochimsen and Johann Wilhelm Gaddum all said there was no reason to increase German interest rates now. \par \par \uc2  The mark strenghtened Monday and Tuesday on expectations that German interest rates would soon rise, converging with the rest of Europe before monetary union on Jan. 1. \par \par \uc2  Also quashing such speculation late Tuesday, Italy trimmed a key lending rate, indicating yields elsewhere in Europe could move downward to converge with Germany, rather than the reverse. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the pound fell to $1.6717 from $1.6748 at Tuesday's close. The dollar rose to 1.4860 Swiss francs from 1.4815 and to Canadian $1.4324 from C$1.4313. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4m07058\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
