\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's Sakakibara drops hint on U.S. Treasuries. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 1998\par 12:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 22 (Reuters) - Influential finance bureaucrat Eisuke Sakakibara hinted on Wednesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stood ready to sell U.S. Treasuries to raise \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds - a comment seen as a sign of Tokyo's growing frustration with the markets. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters after a symposium that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had ample foreign reserves to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Asked about the possibility of selling U.S. Treasuries to fund \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Sakakibara said jokingly: "Well, you know, we can sell them anytime." \par \par \uc2  Japan is the largest single international holder of U.S. Treasury securities, with about 25 percent of U.S. debt. These holdings are closely tracked by the markets. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said it was important to convince the world that Japanese assets were a "buy" and added that he believed the U.S. stock market would "peak out", although he did not give a specific time frame. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara's comments gradually pushed the dollar down by nearly one yen from its Wednesday high, and it was trading at 130.65/70 in early European trade. The U.S. Treasuries market had shown little reaction to the comment by late Tokyo trading. \par \par \uc2  Many Tokyo traders said Sakakibara's comments appeared to reflect his displeasure and irritation at the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's reluctance to push up the yen despite a Group of Seven (G7) communique last week which welcomed Japan's steps to stimulate demand and correct an excessively weak yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose decisively above 130 yen after the G7 meeting and has remained well-bid since then. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily ahead of the G7, selling dollars for yen in New York on April 9 and the next day in Tokyo, hammering down the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down to about 127.40 yen. Kazunori Iida, dealing section manager at Nissho Iwai Corp, said: "The way he (Sakakibara) spoke today was strong and he seemed quite unhappy (with the yen's recent falls)." \par \par \uc2  Iida said it was natural that dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would lead to some sales of U.S. Treasuries. \par \par \uc2  "But a top official like him should not have mentioned it," he added, pointing to its potential market impact. \par \par \uc2  But bond dealers said that the comment by Sakakibara was not as shocking as remarks by Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto last June, which rocked U.S. financial markets. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto said at the time that Japan had in the past been tempted to sell its holdings of U.S. Treasury securities and boost its gold reserves, but had not done so. \par \par \uc2  The comment was taken by U.S. markets as a veiled threat by Hashimoto to sell off Japan's Treasury holdings and sent shockwaves through global markets. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara's comment this time was not expected to undermine the basic sentiment in the Treasury market. \par \par \uc2  However, traders said there is a need to monitor the market closely following widespread talk last week that the Bank of Japan had sold about $12 billion of T-bills. \par \par \uc2  On April 13, Treasury prices sank after the Federal Reserve of New York confirmed it was conducting a "customer-related" transaction. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Fed was selling bills on behalf of an unnamed foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - widely believed to be the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  Traders said it was unusual for the origin of a Fed sale to be immediately known by the market, speculating that the Japan may have wanted its presence known. \par \par \uc2  "Given the fact that the BOJ had been spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and after the rumoured sales of T-Bills, the market was not surprised by Mr Sakakibara's comments," said Kouki Muroi, deputy manager of financial markets trading division at Nippon Credit Bank. \par \par \uc2  "We cannot completely ignore his comments, but I don't think that this will be a major factor to trigger major sales in the Treasury market," Muroi said. \par \par \uc2  Markets will have another chance to tune into to Sakakibara - who is known as "Mr Yen" for his influence on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets - on Thursday, when he speaks again at another symposium, specifically on the subject of the dollar/yen rate. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk 813-3432-8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4m06z26\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
