\par\par \b \uc2  Sakakibara -2: Declines Comment On Concerted  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 135 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 1998\par 02:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--A top official at \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  reiterated Tuesday the ministry's stance that an excessively weak yen is undesirable. \par \par \uc2  Speaking to a group of reporters, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara said: 'There is no doubt that an excessively weak yen leads to growth in Japan's current account surplus. So, a weak yen is undesirable.' \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara declined to comment on the recent meeting with monetary officials from the Group of Seven (G-7) leading industrialized nations and wouldn't respond to questions concerning the possibility of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is quoted around Y131.80 Tuesday morning, compared with around Y132.10 late Monday in New York. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 21-04-98 \par \par \uc2  0018GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du4l03ard\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
