\par\par \b \uc2  Weaker dlr/mark partly recovers at Europe midday. \b0\par\par\uc2 692 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 21 (Reuters) - Profit-taking helped a weaker dollar/mark partly recover by midday on Tuesday after the unit set a 2-1/2 month low around 1.7877 marks. \par \par \uc2  Traders expect the partial rebound to be temporary and that the dollar's recovery will fail at a key support level, now turned resistance, around 1.7950 marks. \par \par \uc2  "We broke a couple of key levels on the way down and it's obviously just a bit of profit-taking from dollars sold earlier," said Nick Probert head of spot trading at Bank of America in London. "But it still looks soft and I imagine it's going to go lower a little later in the day." \par \par \uc2  At 1100 GMT dollar/mark was trading around 1.7934/39 from 1.8022/27 in late European trading on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was buoyed by comments from Bundesbank board member Edgar Meister, who said that he saw no urgent need for frantic activity now to bring European union interest rates closer together for the launch of the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Economists said Meister may be seeking to keep the Bundesbank's monetary policy decisions separate from the political aspects of EMU as the May \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  summit approaches. European Union ministers at that meeting will decide EMU's founding members and set \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  conversion rates. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the Bundesbank received some slight evidence of a strengthening economy out of March money supply data reported on Tuesday. But they said the domestic economy still does not support a near-term rise in rates unless it was taken with the aim of narrowing the interest rate gap between Germany and its potential EMU partners. \par \par \uc2  "It's not a situation where domestically the Bundesbank has no reason to raise rates, but it would look political," said Elizabeth Legge, chief economist at Bankgesellschaft Berlin in London. "If they want to do it, they are going to have to wait a bit longer." \par \par \uc2  The dollar began its slide lower in early trading on Tuesday before the Bundesbank reported German March M3 money supply growth of 5.1 percent from 2.8 percent in February. The report far outstripped forecasts and came uncomfortably close to the ceiling of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's three-to-six percent target range. \par \par \uc2  "It seemed a bit strange that there was pretty consistent selling (of dollars) out of Germany ahead of the M3 numbers and it seemed to peter out after the Ifo numbers," said one corporate trader at a U.S. bank in London. \par \par \uc2  The Ifo economic institute's West German business climate index was unchanged at 98.8 compared to expectations of 98.9. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was restrained on concerns the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might seek to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on dollar/yen coincident with details on Friday of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest economic stimulus package. Speculation of further fiscal reform measures also kept dollar bulls at bay. \par \par \uc2  At 1100 GMT dollar/yen was trading at 131.54/66 compared with 132.27/37 in late European trading on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs Eisuke Sakakibara said on Tuesday that excessive yen weakness is undesirable. Sakakibara is scheduled to give a speech on Wednesday and appear as a panellist for a discussion on the dollar Thursday. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, former prime minister Noboru Takeshita argued against revisions to the fiscal reform law, being considered by current Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto. \par \par \uc2  The law requires the government to halve the budget deficit to within three percent of gross domestic product and eliminate deficit-financing bonds by fiscal 2003/04. \par \par \uc2  But financial market participants are concerned that if the government does not extend that target date, it will be unable to make income tax cuts promised by Hashimoto, permanent. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto is to announce his decision on what changes will be made to the fiscal reform law at a meeting on Wednesday of his Fiscal Structural Reform Council, of which Takeshita is a member. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4l073yl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
