\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar, stg give way to firmer mark. \b0\par\par\uc2 733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 21 (Reuters) - The dollar and sterling were lower against the mark in choppy European trade, but dealers were reluctant to call this a reversal in dollar and sterling strength just yet. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark tested a 2-1/2 month low against the mark in early trade as technical supports gave way and sterling/mark slipped to six-week lows, flirting with the key 3.00 mark support level. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the story is strong enough to call an end to the dollar trend at the moment," said Jeremy Stretch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at NatWest Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  "The German numbers are getting a little better and almost everyone is getting more bullish on the German economy. But that shouldn't suggest the Bundesbank is going to automatically push rates higher," said NatWest's Stretch. \par \par \uc2  German March M3 money supply grew a stronger-than-expected annualised 5.1 percent against 2.8 percent in February, proving a drag on dollar/mark in early trade. But Germany's Ifo business climate index for March came in steady at 98.8, just below forecast, which helped dollar/mark recover from early losses. \par \par \uc2  By 1440 GMT, dollar/mark stood at 1.7910/15, up from its early 2-1/2 month low of 1.7877 marks. \par \par \uc2  The Ifo survey served to dampen slightly market nerves over near-term Bundesbank rate action, but comments from Bundesbank member Edgar Meister poured cold water on such speculation. \par \par \uc2  Meister said he saw no need for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to change monetary policy at the moment. He rejected the idea Europe's core low-yielding countries raise rates to bridge the gap with high-yielders and said there was no hectic action needed on economic and monetary union interest rate convergence. \par \par \uc2  "The worry for the Bundesbank is not what's going on in its own backyard, but the overheating out in EMU-land," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  As well as interest rate speculation, the dollar was dogged by its early break below technical support around 1.7950 marks, an area which capped dollar/mark later in the session. \par \par \uc2  "In the short-term, it looks like the dollar has topped out and this perception has seen some frustrated dollar longs shaken out," said on UK bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  It was a similar story with sterling/mark which slipped lower below the key, psychological 3.00 marks level. \par \par \uc2  UK March retail price data excluding mortgage interest payments came in at 2.6 percent year-on-year as forecast but still just above the government's 2.5 percent target. \par \par \uc2  This was not enough to stir sterling bulls and, as with dollar/mark, frustrated sterling longs went looking for the exit. Dealers said the market has long discounted another, small UK interest rate rise and is taking on board the notion the UK rate cycle may have already peaked. \par \par \uc2  "The market is perhaps getting too carried away with interest rate sentiment. It will only take a strong number this week to see sterling reverse again," said NatWest's Stretch. \par \par \uc2  This week sees UK March unemployment and earnings data on Wednesday, March retail sales on Thursday and preliminary first quarter GDP data on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling/mark stood at 2.9978/88 in late Europe, well below early highs around 3.02 marks and late Monday's 3.0258/63 marks. \par \par \uc2  Cable ended a choppy session at $1.6760/70, little changed from $1.6765/75 Monday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, dollar/yen was easier on the day amid the generally softer dollar as well as market caution ahead of details of Japan's latest economic package due at the end of this week. \par \par \uc2  Although the negative bias for the yen has not diminished, the market is still mindful of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen and dollar gains have been limited. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen stood at 131.18/28 in late trade compared with 132.27/37 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Mark/yen was above its lows and was looking to retest the 3-1/2 month peak of 73.53 seen in earlier trade. By late trade, it was changing hands at 73.45/47 per mark from 73.38/43 Monday. ((Andrew Reierson, London newsroom +44 171 542 6745, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4l06x95\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
