\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar solid above 132 yen in subdued midday Tokyo. \b0\par\par\uc2 548 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab  0300 GMT\tab      TRADED HIGH/LOW AS OF 0300 GMT\par  USD/JPY     132.16/18\tab \tab    132.50-131.84\par  USD/DEM     1.8093/95\tab \tab    1.8120-1.8082\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2      TOKYO, April 20 (Reuters) - The dollar was solid above 132 \par \par \uc2  yen at midday on Monday on sporadic buying by investment trust \par \par \uc2  firms, but trade was directionless and mired in a narrow range. \par \par \uc2       Many operators were loath to open large positions amid a \par \par \uc2  lack of fresh factors and lingering caution over possible Bank \par \par \uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  near 133 yen. The market's focus \par \par \uc2  was on details of an economic package expected on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "People took a breather today after volatile trade around the G7 meeting last week," said a dealer at a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  "Both operators who won and those who lost money last week are reluctant to commit themselves in active trade because of a lack of fresh factors," he added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's firmness was not softened by the release of Japan's trade surplus for February. The surplus jumped 56.5 percent from a year earlier to 1.25 trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  "Since we had anticipated a large (surplus) number after Friday's U.S. trade data, the impact of Japan's data was limited," said the Japanese bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters had on average forecast the surplus would rise 53 percent from a year earlier to 1.22 trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  The contentious trade surplus with the United States rose 19 percent compared with a year earlier to 449.24 billion yen. \par \par \uc2  Japan's trade surplus was unlikely to expand drastically in the medium to longer term, although it might continue to rise for a while, an official of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) said. \par \par \uc2  Separately, senior MOF official Haruhiko Kuroda told reporters on Monday he was strongly concerned about the fall in the yen. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said a fall in the yen was not desirable as it would make a decline in the surplus difficult, even if Japan's economy recovered. "We will take decisive action on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  as needed," he added. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at a trust bank said Kuroda's remarks capped the dollar's gains, since they rekindled concerns over possible dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  Traders said stop-loss dollar buy orders were seen in place at around 132.50/60 yen, but that even if such stop-loss buying was triggered, the dollar's gains would be limited to near 133 yen. \par \par \uc2  Traders were unsure as to how the dollar would react to the forthcoming details of Japan's latest economic stimulus package, which are considered unlikely to include surprises. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think that a lack of surprises would lead to a quick yen fall to 134 yen," said one bank trader. Others said such a lack might spark sales of the yen and Japanese stocks. \par \par \uc2  The upcoming stimulus package is to include 10 trillion yen in new fiscal stimulus, of which four trillion yen will consist of income tax cuts over the next two years. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 131.75/85 yen and 1.8088/93 marks in New York on Friday. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk 81-3-3432-1396 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument 