\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr/yen steady by Europe midday but seen rising. \b0\par\par\uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 20 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar held steady at midday in Europe against the yen and mark as the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  capped dollar/yen. \par \par \uc2  But economists said the threat was merely a temporary restraint for a dollar headed towards 135 yen and perhaps even 145 in the next few months. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has capped dollar/yen around these levels for the last two sessions," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in London. But she maintained a six-month forecast for dollar/yen of 145 yen. \par \par \uc2  "All they can hope to do is stall the pace of the dollar." \par \par \uc2  At 1015 GMT dollar/yen was holding firm to its early European levels around 132.25/35, down from its Asian highs but stronger than the 131.70/80 it traded at late in Europe on Friday. Dollar/mark held steady near early levels around 1.8108/13 marks against 1.8075/80 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the most recent threat of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  emerged overnight as dollar/yen climbed to an 11-day high around 132.44. \par \par \uc2  During the session, Haruhiko Kuroda, the director-general of the International Finance Bureau of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said he was strongly concerned about the yen's fall and that Japan would take "decisive action on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ". \par \par \uc2  The threat of dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  led yen bears to look for other units to buy against Japan's ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said interest to buy mark/yen had been restraining the dollar's strength against the mark. \par \par \uc2  In the week since the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  began its latest round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , mark/yen has risen to 73.18 yen on Friday from a one-month low of 70.06 seen on April 13 and threatens to exceed a three-month high of 73.25 set on April 7. \par \par \uc2  "It is having this peculiar secondary effect, although dollar/mark should trend lower anyway," said Tony Norfield, treasury economist at ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  Norfield also said sterling/yen was stronger as a result of the yen's overall weakness and the pound's attraction as a high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Chartists said they were targeting a multi-year top over 225 for sterling/yen, while cable hit a five-month peak of $1.6972 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling/mark, which has fallen 2.5 percent from the 8-1/2-year high hit on March 31, was trading at 3.0320/30 against 3.0455/65 on Friday. Cable was at $1.6736/46 against $1.6850/60. \par \par \uc2  Britain reported its public sector borrowing requirement was at 7.2 billion pounds in March, bringing the full-year deficit to 922 million, its lowest since 1990/91. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Japan's politically sensitive customs-cleared trade surplus for March jumped 57 percent from a year earlier to 1.25 trillion yen, revealing the effects of a weaker yen. The data showed an 11 percent plunge in imports in value terms, while exports rose one percent. \par \par \uc2  Japan's trade surplus remains a sensitive issue as trade with crisis-hit Asian countries falls and cheaper Japanese goods nudge out those of its competitors in Europe and North America. \par \par \uc2  Japan has come under heavy pressure to boost its domestic demand and open its markets further to absorb more imports from its troubled Asian neighbours. \par \par \uc2  In Europe, the euro came to the fore after the International Herald Tribune reported that a London-based study showed Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would probably raise European living standards, possibly at the expense of the United States. \par \par \uc2  The newspaper quoted the study as saying the shock of the euro launch "is likely to be substantial and relatively sudden". \par \par \uc2  The Bundesbank also made a reference to European monetary union in its monthly report for April, saying EMU would increase the need for clear stability oriented and long-term fiscal and monetary policies to protect financial markets from turbulence. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom, 44-171-542-8968)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4k074o6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
