\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr capped by  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  threat in early Europe. \b0\par\par\uc2 800 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 20 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar started firmer against the yen and mark in Europe on Monday, but off its Asian highs amid concerns that the Bank of Japan could wade in to cap further gains. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was staying above 132 yen in European trade with a cap around its Asian high of 132.44, where some reckon the Bank of Japan might wade into the market to prop up the faltering yen with a round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "There is still very much a threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Tony Norfield, treasury economist at ABN Amro. "Nearly everybody is negative on yen though, it just depends how brave you are." \par \par \uc2  At 0750 GMT dollar/yen was trading around 132.18/23, off its Asian highs, but stronger than the 131.70/80 levels it traded at in late Europe on Friday. Dollar/mark edged up to 1.8103/08 marks from 1.8075/80 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  But Norfield warned that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  without further structural reform to the Japanese financial system would only serve to slow the dollar's rise in the longer term. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, Japanese Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga said the impact of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was weakened by U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin's comments in support of a strong dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Immediately after we took steps to correct the excessive yen weakness, Secretary Rubin made a statement to support Japan's action," Matsunaga told a parliamentary committee. \par \par \uc2  Matsunaga went on to say a Rubin remark on a different occasion that a strong dollar was in the U.S. interest had weakened the effect of Japan's action to correct the yen's "excessive weakness". \par \par \uc2  "Maybe they were expecting more bang for their buck and this may reflect some disappointment that Mr Rubin did not support them more," said David Coleman, chief economist at CIBC Wood Gundy in London. \par \par \uc2  Coleman said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fiscal packages and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be enough without continued stuctural reform to the nation's financial institutions. \par \par \uc2  "Signalling a willingness to embark on that will go a long way in the financial markets," Coleman said. "That may actually be more important than the packages." \par \par \uc2  Japan's politically sensitive customs-cleared trade surplus for March jumped 57 percent from a year earlier to 1.25 trillion yen, revealing the effects of a weaker yen. The data showed an 11 percent plunge in imports in value terms, while exports rose one percent. \par \par \uc2  Japan's trade surplus remains a sensitive issue as trade with crisis-hit Asian countries falls and cheaper Japanese goods nudge out those of its competitors in Europe and North America. \par \par \uc2  Japan has come under heavy pressure to boost its domestic demand and open its markets further to absorb more imports from its troubled Asian neighbours. \par \par \uc2  In Europe, the euro came to the fore again after the International Herald Tribune reported that a London-based study showed Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would probably raise European living standards, possibly at the expense of diminished prosperity in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  The newspaper quoted the study as saying the shock of the euro's launch "is likely to be substantial and relatively sudden". \par \par \uc2  Renewed market interest in European economic and monetary union (EMU) was spurred by comments from the Bundesbank on Monday. Germany's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in its April monthly report that EMU would increase the need for clear stability-oriented and long-term fiscal and monetary policies to protect financial markets from turbulence. \par \par \uc2  There was also interest in the sharply weaker Australian dollar after the unit slid three-quarters of a U.S. cent lower on the day in Asian trading. \par \par \uc2  By 0845 GMT, the Aussie dollar was at US$0.6458/65, above its earlier lows of $0.6454 but well off the $0.6534/39 seen late on Friday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  All of the damage came early in Asian trading after forecaster Access Economics spooked the market by warning that the current account deficit could blow out to "banana republic" proportions to six percent of gross domestic product next year. \par \par \uc2  The widening of the current account deficit was not fresh news, with a recent Reuters poll finding a median forecast of a deficit of 5.6 percent of GDP next year, but Access Economics' use of the "banana republic" reference shook the market. \par \par \uc2  The use of the phrase "banana republic" is a reference to a comment in the mid-1980s by then-Australian treasurer Paul Keating, whose comments at the time triggered a six cent plunge in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4k07136\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
