\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar trapped below 132 yen at midday Europe. \b0\par\par\uc2 730 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 17 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar remained trapped below 132 yen at midday in Europe on rumours that a sustained breach of that level would prompt a Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the ailing Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The 132 area attracted sellers on Thursday and proved to be sticky again on Friday as talk circulated of selling interest by a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  around that level. Concern the BOJ might sell around 132 led yen bears to sell yen on the crosses instead. \par \par \uc2  "There is some talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the 132 level," said Mark Geddes, treasury economist at ABN AMRO. "But the market still wants to sell the yen." \par \par \uc2  At 0954 GMT dollar/yen was trading at 131.46/56 compared to an overnight high of 132.18 and 131.72/82 in late European trading on Thursday. Dollar/mark slipped to 1.8009/19 from earlier highs of 1.8079 and Thursday's 1.8085/90. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar shrugged off comments from senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda, who said that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is always possible. His comments followed a statement by Japanese Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga that there were no discussions regarding coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in meetings he had with U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin or with the Group of Seven (G7) on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also said long dollar holders began to square their positions ahead of U.S. February international trade data due out at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The market will be closely watching the trade figures for signs of the effects a stronger dollar has had on the nation's trade gap as U.S. export prices rise in comparison with their trading partners. \par \par \uc2  The Asian crisis is expected to exacerbate the U.S. trade deficit this year by eroding U.S. net exports. \par \par \uc2  Japan's trade surplus with the U.S., a thorny political issue, has ballooned due to the weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  An average of analysts' forecasts point to a February trade deficit of $11.8 billion from January's $12.0 billion shortfall. \par \par \uc2  "Going into the numbers the market is probably squaring long dollar positions, but if it is in line or better than expectations than that would clearly be dollar positive," ABN AMRO's Geddes said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the 132-yen cap on dollar/yen pushed yen bears to sell yen against sterling and mark today. \par \par \uc2  "If you look particularly at mark/yen there is a lot of upside potential there," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  At midday Europe sterling/yen had slipped back on profit-taking to 222.04/34 yen after hitting highs around 223.67, while mark/yen was off highs of 73.18 yen at 72.92/95 yen, but up from 72.86/89 in late European trading on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Mark/yen interest weighed on dollar/mark alongside longer-term expectations of a possible rise in German interest rates. The Bundesbank left interest rates unchanged at its regular policy meeting on Friday, but some analysts said they expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates by June. \par \par \uc2  "In the next four to six weeks I think we'll get a repo rate hike in Germany," said Barrow at Bear Stearns. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank President Hans Tietmeyer put speculation about a rise at today's meeting to rest by saying a large liquidity drain at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repo tender this Wednesday was not a signal of a future rate rise. \par \par \uc2  "We are not signalling any tightening for the time being, but of course we are monitoring the situation very closely and that means, of course, we have to see what is going on in the next month," Tietmeyer said in an interview with Reuters Financial Television. \par \par \uc2  Market focus is also on U.S. March capacity utilisation at 1315 GMT, seen dipping to 82.4 percent from 82.7 in February. \par \par \uc2  U.S. March industrial production, which is also due at 1315 GMT, is seen unchanged from the previous month. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. preliminary April Michigan survey, due at 1400 GMT, is expected to come in at 107.4 compared with 106.5 in March. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4h0765t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
