\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar off highs in morning Europe on BOJ concern. \b0\par\par\uc2 659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 17 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped off overnight highs above 132 yen in morning Europe on Friday, retreating amid concern that higher levels would spur the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said a stronger dollar was nudged off its highs after comments from Eisuke Sakakibara, one of Japan's top international monetary policy officials, heightened concerns the BOJ could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the dollar's rise. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar does look offered at 132 and obviously that (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is at the backs of peoples' minds," said Nick Probert, head of spot trading at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "I have grave concern over the recent development on the exchange rate market," Sakakibara, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters at a meeting of the International Monetary Fund in Washington. \par \par \uc2  The yen was also helped by U.S. rating agency Standard & Poor's denial of a market rumour that it was considering a negative rating action against Japan. \par \par \uc2  At 0740 GMT dollar/yen was trading at 131.80/90 compared to an overnight high of 132.18 and 131.72/82 in late European trading on Thursday. Dollar/mark slipped to 1.8026/36 from Thursday's 1.8085/90. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga added to the market's wariness on Friday. He said Japan and the U.S. had agreed to keep in contact to deal appropriately with excessive yen weakness which could lead to an increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current account surplus. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears on dollar/yen resulted in more interest in selling the yen on the crosses, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Sterling/yen rose to 223.34/65 yen from Thursday's late European levels of 222.65/96. \par \par \uc2  The mark climbed to 73.13/16 yen from 72.86/89 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "While the market wants to sell the yen it is getting too dangerous to buy dollar/yen, so the (yen) weakness is coming out in mark/yen and the crosses," said one trader at a major U.S. bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark slipped back towards 1.80 ahead of news from Friday's Bundesbank council meeting amid fading speculation that Germany's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could decide to raise interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank President Hans Tietmeyer attempted to put the speculation to rest by saying a large liquidity drain at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repo tender this Wednesday was not a signal of a future rate rise. \par \par \uc2  "We are not signalling any tightening for the time being, but of course we are monitoring the situation very closely and that means, of course, we have to see what is going on in the next month," Tietmeyer said in an interview with Reuters Financial Television. \par \par \uc2  The market will be closely watching U.S. international trade data due at 1230 GMT for signs of the effects a stronger dollar has had on the nation's trade gap as U.S. export prices rise in comparison with their trading partners. \par \par \uc2  The Asian crisis is expected to exacerbate the U.S. trade deficit this year by eroding U.S. net exports. \par \par \uc2  Japan's trade surplus with the U.S., a thorny political issue, has ballooned due to the weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters survey of economists forecast a narrowing in the U.S. trade gap to $11.8 billion from January's $12 billion deficit. \par \par \uc2  Market pundits also believe U.S. March capacity utilisation, due at 1315 GMT, will dip to 82.4 percent from 82.7 percent in February. \par \par \uc2  U.S. March industrial production, which is also due at 1315 GMT, is seen unchanged from the previous month. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. preliminary April Michigan survey, due at 1400 GMT, is expected to come in at 107.4 compared with 106.5 in March. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4h075x6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
