\par\par \b \uc2  Tsys Shrug Off Strong Claims; Mkt Mostly Up; 30Y Up 10/32 \b0\par\par\uc2 474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 1998\par 15:34\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--U.S. Treasurys shrugged off some\par stronger-than-expected economic data early Thursday, as\par dollar strength underpinned gains concentrated in the\par long-end.\par    At 9:20 a.m. EDT, the 30-year bond was up 10/32 at 103\par 22/32 to yield 5.88%. At the Chicago Board of Trade, the\par March bond contract is up 11/32 at 121-12.\par    "The dollar's the story here," said Nikko Securities\par Senior Economist Dan Seto. "The G-7's over, with no\par agreement on coordination, so now the pressure is on Japan"\par to improve their economic fundamentals to support a stronger\par yen.\par    The market "opened higher on the dollar," said one\par trader, adding that the market has been "by and large\par unchanged" since the opening.\par    The U.S. dollar saw gains late Wednesday and Thursday as\par the Group of Seven finance ministers meeting in Washington\par did not yield any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    However, observers said concern that the high dollar may\par spark \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be pressuring\par the short-end.\par    The Treasurys market dipped briefly Thursday morning, as\par the Labor Department reported jobless claims fell 22,000 to\par 289,000 the week ended April 11. The Dow Jones\par Newswires-CNBC consensus forecast had called for a 2,000\par increase to 310,000.\par    The Commerce Department reported March housing starts\par were at a 1.59 million seasonally adjusted annual rate, down\par 2.8% from February's revised figure. March's figure was near\par the consensus forecast of a 1.58 million rate.\par    At 10:00 A.M. EDT, the Federal Reserve Bank of\par Philadephia is scheduled to release its April business\par outlook survey. The survey is the first look at\par manufacturing activity in April.\par    A steady stream of Fed and Treasury officials are set to\par deliver remarks Thursday. Fed Governor Edward Gramlich is\par scheduled to deliver remarks in Oklahoma City at 1:30 P.M.\par EDT; Fed Governor Laurence Meyer is in Pennsylvania at 2:00\par P.M. EDT; and Fed Governor Roger Ferguson is set to deliver\par remarks in New York at 7:30 P.M. EDT.\par    Treasury Secretary is scheduled to deliver opening\par remarks at the meeting of the Group of 22 finance ministers\par and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors in Washington at 6:00 P.M. EDT.\par    5 1/2%     2-Year  99  30/32    Unch       5.52%\par    5 1/2%     5-year  99  23/32    Unch       5.56%\par    5 1/2%    10-Year  99  12/32    Up  2/32   5.58%\par    6 1/8%    30-Year  103 22/32    Up 10/32   5.86%\par    2-30-Year yield Spread 34 BPS Vs 35 BPS\par  -By Brian Blackstone; 201-938-4156\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  04-16-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0934EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1079 4041 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du4g00xcu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
