\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr/yen ends US flat, fails to extend Asian gains. \b0\par\par\uc2 617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, April 16 (Reuters) - The dollar ended unchanged against the yen after jumping overnight and slipped against the mark as sentiment appeared to be shifting in favor of some European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Trading was generally quiet however as investors digested Wednesday's Group of Seven communique, which came after New York trading had officially closed, and caught their breath after recent nervous and sometimes volatile trading sessions. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen closed at 131.70/80, unchanged from its opening level and dollar/mark ended at 1.8040/50, down from its opening level at 1.8085/95. \par \par \uc2  One day after the Group of Seven's finance ministers and central bankers urged Japan to boost its economy, U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin warned Tokyo must generate new growth as Asia could not recover with Japan's economic health in doubt. \par \par \uc2  "Japan must generate substantial growth to help maintain a growing world economy and absorb a growing share of imports from emerging markets," Rubin said in Washington. \par \par \uc2  Traders, newly confident the United States and its partners would not join forces with Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push the dollar lower through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , again sold yen for dollars overnight. \par \par \uc2  "The G7 nations said there is a possibility they could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the reality is that they are not going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the dollar is going higher," said Chris Melendez, senior dealer as Sanwa Bank California. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended trading nearly two yen above Wednesday's 130 yen closing rate, with dealers saying gains might be limited now as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fresh from heavy dollar sales last week, could still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  While \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Economic Planning Agency Minister Koji Omi said the government would announce a package of economic stimulus measures before April 25, dealers remained skeptical. \par \par \uc2  "We should see the dollar trading between 125 and 135 yen until we see some independent strength from the Japanese economy," Mike Malpede, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Refco Group Ltd. said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar gained slightly versus the German mark as analysts said Wednesday's talk the Bundesbank might be planning to raise interest rates was premature. \par \par \uc2  Considering Germany's 12.1 percent unemployment rate and low inflation, analysts said the Bundesbank would likely delay any interest rates hikes. They noted rates may rise later in the year as European rates converge ahead of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  union. \par \par \uc2  But dealers did say that the dollar's bull run against the mark might soon be ended as sentiment in Europe appeared to be improving and investors in Asia and possibly the U.S. might be looking for new investment opportunities if the U.S. stock market comes off its highs. \par \par \uc2  "Some reversal of the dollar's bull trend against the mark is possible and the trade is really looking for catalyst of signal. Friday's U.S. trade numbers could be very important for the dollar outlook," Malpede said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a break below 1.7900 marks could usher in new trading range for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  In other trading the British pound gained against the dollar rising to $1.6900/10 after opening at 1.6850/60, as sterling continues to reflect worries about European monetary union and the potential for a UK rate hike. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1.5028/38 Swiss francs, modestly below its morning level of 1.5050/55. It also fell against the Canadian dollar to C$1.4325/35 after opening at C$1.4380/85. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar closed at $0.6517/22 up from its $0.6500/05 opening level. \par \par \uc2  ((-Svea Herbst-Bayliss, NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1638 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4g07dnl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
