\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Dollar gyrates, Tokyo shares sink after G7. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 1998\par 04:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 16 (Reuters) - The dollar gyrated in Tokyo trade on Thursday as talk of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was overwhelmed by perceptions that the Group of Seven (G7) rich nations would not act jointly to support the yen. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo share prices slid sharply as investors - like the G7 - fretted about Japan's economy and its policies. \par \par \uc2  The 225-share Nikkei average ended down 2.55 percent, or 415.53 points, at 15,883.77. The steep fall in Tokyo share prices sapped support for the yen, which was trading weakly at about 131.35 yen to the dollar in late Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the G7 finance ministers and central bankers had trod a fine line in Washington on Wednesday in an effort to keep the yen from further steep slides without prompting an equally steep rise in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "There appears to be a broad consensus that they didn't want to see a collapse in the yen," said Russell Jones, chief economist at Lehman Brothers in Tokyo. "On the other hand, they don't want to create an exaggerated appreciation either." \par \par \uc2  "They are treading a narrow line on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the moment," he added. \par \par \uc2  In a communique issued after their meeting in Washington, the G7 said: "We emphasised that it is important to avoid excessive (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) depreciation where this could exacerbate large external imbalances." Those comments echoed closely a statement issued at their previous February meeting. \par \par \uc2  But the statement added a sentence urging Japan to implement quickly a strong programme of fiscal steps and structural reforms, which would also help correct the yen's "excessive depreciation". \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo the dollar first rose more than a yen from its New York close to above 131 yen, as some dealers said the G7 had done the minimum needed to prevent a sharp yen fall but not agreed the need for joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar then fell sharply on talk of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but rebounded after a lack of follow-through sales raised doubts over whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had really acted. \par \par \uc2  While U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin told a news conference that joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not been discussed, senior Japanese finance official Eisuke Sakakibara insisted the G7 statement left the door open to coordinated action. \par \par \uc2  "The meaning of that is that it is indicating the possibility of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Sakakibara told a news conference in Washington. "G7 countries share the strong concern over the excessive depreciation of the yen." \par \par \uc2  A Japanese finance official later told reporters in Washington that the G7 saw the current yen level as too weak and that Washington and Tokyo shared the view that a rate of 130 yen to the dollar was excessive. \par \par \uc2  A stronger yen, the Japanese official added, was good for the United States, Japan and other Asian nations. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto also weighed in, telling reporters on Thursday that the communique showed the G7 would support Japan's efforts to prevent the yen from falling too much, Jiji news agency reported. \par \par \uc2  Some economists echoed that assessment. \par \par \uc2  "They really don't want to see a further devaluation of the yen...because it unsettles Asia," said Bronwyn Curtis, chief international economist at Nomura. \par \par \uc2  Curtis added that joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was possible, but only if authorities felt that conditions were ripe for success. "If they all go in together, they have to be successful," she said. \par \par \uc2  As if to underscore a comment by Rubin that the markets would be the final judge of Japanese policy, Tokyo share prices sank below the key 16,000 level, with participants glum about the economy and government plans. \par \par \uc2  Like Japan's G7 colleagues, Tokyo stock market players, as well as Japanese business leaders, want quick and decisive official action. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto said last week he would put his budget deficit-cutting effort on the back burner and implement an extra 4 trillion yen ($30.5 billion) in income tax cuts as part of a package officials say will include more than 10 trillion yen in real stimulus. \par \par \uc2  Critics, however, were disappointed by the government's failure to make the tax cuts permanent and speed up reductions in corporate tax rates. \par \par \uc2  The head of the government's tax panel, Hiroshi Kato, told Reuters Financial Television on Thursday Japan is unlikely to decide this year whether to make the income tax cuts permanent. ($1=131 yen) ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8018 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4g07c8j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
