\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr/yen rally pauses for breath at Europe midday. \b0\par\par\uc2 414 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 16 (Reuters) - A strong overnight rally in dollar/yen paused for a breath by midday in Europe on concerns that higher levels could prompt the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar rose after a G7 communique stopped short of endorsing concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen. \par \par \uc2  By 1000 GMT the dollar had retreated to 131.35/45 yen from earlier session highs of 131.65 after a rise of more than two yen from Wednesday's lows of 128.88 yen. \par \par \uc2  "I think it's mainly concerns the Bank of Japan will step in above these levels," said Sandy Batten, senior economist at Citibank in London. \par \par \uc2  But Batten said the market bias to push dollar/yen higher would emerge again in spite of the threat of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and until the full measure of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's most recent economic stimulus package is revealed and the effects can be gauged. \par \par \uc2  "We need to see some fiscal policy improvement rather than just talk from policymakers," Batten said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Economic Planning Agency Minister Koji Omi told reporters on Thursday that the Japanese government wants to announce its package of economic stimulus measures before he meets with the Organisation for Economic Co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Development (OECD) on April 25. \par \par \uc2  The dollar firmed against the mark by midday on Thursday to 1.8090/08 from 1.7997/02 in late European trading on Wednesday after speculation eased that the Bundesbank might raise rates at Friday's council meeting. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark slipped on Wednesday after the Bundesbank surprised players by draining rather than adding funds to the money market. \par \par \uc2  "People are just sort of jumping the gun," said Elizabeth Legge, chief economist at Bankgesellschaft Berlin in London. "There are no domestic reasons for raising rates. The only reason you could put forward is the European dimension." \par \par \uc2  Legge said the Bundesbank has said it will consider the wider implications of its monetary policies, including the economic circumstances of other European countries, at its regular Bundesbank council meetings. \par \par \uc2  But she said Germany's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it will not begin considering those factors until the second half of the year. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll of 10 economists showed a unanimous view that the Bundesbank would not raise rates at Friday's meeting. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4g07bhu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
