\par\par \b \uc2  Post-G7 dollar rally to fade ahead of 135 yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Casciato \par \uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 16 (Reuters) - The dollar's rally against the yen, after a G7 communique containing no endorsement of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is expected to fade before reaching last week's highs over 135 yen. \par \par \uc2  Fear of another combination of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fiscal measures out of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month is expected to keep dollar bulls from achieving last week's 6-1/2 year highs. \par \par \uc2  "There could be an announcement on April 25 and 135 is quite ambitious given the threat it will coincide with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Michael Lewis, economist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell. \par \par \uc2  Last week, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively to support the yen after the government announced some details of its most recent economic stimulus package. The BOJ action pushed the dollar to lows around 127.35 yen on Friday from its 135.42 Y 6-1/2 year high seen at the start of April. \par \par \uc2  Deutsche's Lewis said the market expects the Bank of Japan will seek to knock the dollar back again when Japan announces further details of its latest stimulus measures this month. \par \par \uc2  At 0800 GMT dollar/yen had firmed to 131.34/44 yen from 129.50/60 in late European trading on Wednesday before the G7 delivered its communiqui. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, the Economic Planning Agency Minister Koji Omi told reporters on Thursday that the Japanese government wants to announce its package of economic stimulus measures before April 25, when he will meet with the Organisation for Economic Co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Development (OECD). \par \par \uc2  Economists said that despite getting more package details, they saw no significant improvement in Japan's economic outlook and further yen depreciation was expected. \par \par \uc2  "Our six-month forecast for dollar/yen is 145 yen and we have a weakening trend to 150 yen at the end of 12 months," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in London. \par \par \uc2  Foley said the likelihood the G7 would endorse concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to turn dollar/yen around was slim as long as Japan's economic fundamentals fail to support the case for a stronger yen. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan said on Monday that Japan's economy remained stagnant and there remained strong downward pressure on economic activities as a whole. \par \par \uc2  "Japan's economic data continues to show the economy getting weaker," Foley said. "Unless the fundamental economic backdrop improves they (the G7) know that concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be just a whistle in the wind." \par \par \uc2  Other economists took a more positive view of the yen from the results of the G7 meeting, saying the wording of the communiqui suggested further fiscal and structural reform could be rewarded with G7 endorsement of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "What the G7 are saying to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is 'you provide some more aggressive fiscal measures and we'll provide the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 '," said Mark Geddes, treasury economist at ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  Haruhiko Kuroda, the director-general of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's International Finance Bureau, said on Thursday that concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was always possible and the markets would understand the strong statement issued by the G7. \par \par \uc2  In the text of the communiqui which deals with exchange rates, the G7 strengthened their February statement by specifically mentioning "excessive depreciation in the yen" and saying it supports Japan for measures to stimulate domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  The February communiqui said: "...it is important to avoid excessive depreciations (in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) where this could exacerbate large external trade balances" and the G7 "agreed to monitor developments in exchange markets and to cooperate as appropriate". \par \par \uc2  ((London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4g07bdt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
