\par\par \b \uc2  Early Tokyo -2: Market To Focus On G-7 Statement On Japan \b0\par\par\uc2 331 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 1998\par 03:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The mark is trading at Y71.71, unchanged from Y71.71 earlier and below Y71.96 in New York late Tuesday. The mark was quoted late in Tokyo Tuesday at Y71.53. \par \par \uc2  Sterling is at $1.6850, up from $1.6845 earlier and unchanged from New York late Tuesday. Sterling was at $1.6671 in Tokyo late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Traders are focusing on how the G-7 statement will assess Japan's Y16 trillion economic stimulus package, announced last week by Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto. \par \par \uc2  Japan's economic recovery is expected to be the number one issue at the G-7 meeting since its recovery is understood to be a key element in helping Asian countries out of their economic troubles. \par \par \uc2  'If other G-7 countries give their approval to the latest Japanese measures, the dollar is likely to further weaken against yen,' Nippon Credit Bank's Imamura said. 'The (U.S.) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may go down to Y127.00 in the near term.' \par \par \uc2  Many traders expect the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will conduct further dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the meeting if the Japanese economic stimulus package is welcomed in the meeting. \par \par \uc2  'The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually times its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with other economic events (such as the G-7 meeting),' said a Tokyo-based trader at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, the dollar is expected to strengthen if the G-7 nations demand further pump-priming steps from Japan. \par \par \uc2  'It's hard to tell whether the recent package has satisfied other countries. If Japan is required to take further (economic) steps, the dollar is likely to rise back to near Y135.00,' said Yukihiko Hashimoto, a senior trader at Sanwa Bank. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is seen trading between Y128.60-Y129.80 and DEM1.7950-DEM1.8050 for the rest of the Asian day. \par \par \uc2  -By Takeshi Takeuchi; 813-5255-2934; ttakeuchi@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 15-04-98 \par \par \uc2  0102GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du4f034fu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
