\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar hovers near key levels midday in Europe. \b0\par\par\uc2 760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 15 (Reuters) - The dollar hovered near 1.80 marks and 130 yen at midday in Europe on Wednesday, restrained by caution ahead of a communiqui from a G7 finance ministers meeting on the Asian crisis in Washington today. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market remained wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and comments in support of the yen after the Bank of Japan blasted the dollar lower by buying yen through the U.S. Federal Reserve on Thursday and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself on Friday in Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is looking a bit shaky, particularly against the yen," said David Coleman, chief economist at CIBC Wood Gundy. "We are on hold for the communiqui." \par \par \uc2  Coleman said the market would be looking to the communique to see if the U.S. and other G7 countries are willing to put their full weight behind a turnaround in dollar/yen, which has risen nearly 25 yen from a low of 110.52 yen on June 10 to a high last week of 135.42 before the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Are they willing to go the final 10 yards and publicly endorse a turnaround in the yen?" Coleman said. "I'm not so sure, given (U.S. Treasury Secretary) Rubin's statements last week that a strong dollar was good for the U.S. economy." \par \par \uc2  At 1000 GMT dollar/yen was at 129.42/52 compared to 129.32/39 on Tuesday, but off overnight highs of 129.76. \par \par \uc2  Last week Rubin welcomed the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but later said a strong dollar was good for the U.S. economy. Rubin has also said G7 policymakers had no intention of easing pressure on Japan to take steps to revive its economy. \par \par \uc2  But on Wednesday a Japanese newspaper reported the United States had put before the G7 - Japan, Britain, Germany, Italy, France, Canada and the U.S. - a draft communique which vowed to take action to encourage \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability and acknowledged the importance of avoiding a further fall in the yen. \par \par \uc2  Such a communique would significantly beef up the G7's last communique and raise the spectre of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But economists said the communique would stop short of delivering a clear directional message to the market. \par \par \uc2  "They will make a bigger effort to ensure the market knows they are interested in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability, but they are not going to start talking the yen up," said Ravi Bulchandani, senior international economist at Morgan Stanley in London. \par \par \uc2  He said that because of the strong, low-inflation economy in the U.S. and the weakened Japanese economy, the economic conditions do not exist for the G7 to apply concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a spark for a turnaround in the dollar's recent gains. \par \par \uc2  The dollar firmed to around 1.8061/66 marks from 1.8033/38 in late European trading on Tuesday, but down from seven-month highs of around 1.8562 marks seen earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  Economists said speculation the Fed would raise U.S. interest rates was receding on continued signs of softening inflationary pressures in the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  They said softer retail sales and flat consumer prices in March, reported on Tuesday, added to the expected easing in inflation pressures from last week's softer jobs numbers. \par \par \uc2  U.S. February business inventories data is due at 1230 GMT, while March import/export prices will be released at 1400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in the European \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the Dutch guilder edged to 112.57/75 per 100 marks from 112.62 in late European trading on Tuesday after the Dutch \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it has no plans to revalue the guilder. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's comment came after speculation the guilder may have to be revalued in the European exchange rate mechanism as the Dutch economy outperforms those of other core European countries. \par \par \uc2  The British pound received a boost after minutes of the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee meeting revealed it was evenly split over a rate move last month. \par \par \uc2  The minutes showed Governor Eddie George had cast the decisive vote to leave rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  As a result sterling/mark was pushed to a session high of 3.0410 marks from 3.0250 just before the minutes were released, and Tuesday's late European levels at 3.0372/82. Cable stood at $1.6835/40 against Tuesday's $1.6844/54. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4f07h46\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
