\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Nervous market buckles down for G7. \b0\par\par\uc2 623 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 15 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market ended a choppy European session on a mixed note on Wednesday as dealers buckled down ahead of the Group of Seven (G7) industrialised nations meeting in Washington. \par \par \uc2  The main focus remained on the yen and what message the G7 was likely to deliver on Japan's faltering economy and weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It's likely the G7 will endorse the Japanese authorities' efforts to stimulate the economy, and there may be increased pressure particularly from Rubin to step up the efforts," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in London. \par \par \uc2  Japan Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga is also due to meet U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin where he is expected to detail Japan's recent economic stimulus package. \par \par \uc2  Analysts in London said the G7 would likely deliver a yen-supportive message but are also likely to push Japan for further reforms in Japan beyond fiscal stimulus packages. \par \par \uc2  A Japanese paper reported overnight that the G7 were considering issuing a communiqui that will vow to take action to encourage \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability and acknowledge the importance of avoiding a fall in the yen. This added to market nerves the G7 was mulling concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the weak yen. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen stood at 129.60/70 at 1520 GMT, up from earlier lows just under 129 but still trapped under the session top of 130 yen. It stood at 129.32/39 late Tuesday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  "We are on hold for the communiqui," said David Coleman, chief economist at CIBC Wood Gundy in London. "The dollar is looking a bit shaky, particularly against the yen." \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury's Rubin said Wednesday said the G7 talks on Japan stimulus measures would be independent of those on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  After last week's bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen, the market remained wary of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action but largely sceptical of concerted G7 action. \par \par \uc2  "There probably won't be any significant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic data keeps looking poor. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't be able to change the weak tone of the yen unless we saw more tax cuts or a better posture in the economy," said Barclay Capital's Foley. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in Europe, dollar/mark slipped in afternoon trade, pressured by some last-minute pre-G7 position squaring and also knocked by speculation the Bundesbank was set to raise its repo rate. But traders said the rate hike talk was likely triggered by an unexpected 1.9 billion mark repo tender drainage, seen as a pre-emptive move ahead of expected cash inflows this week. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark stood at 1.7990/95, up from a session low of 1.7950 marks but still below late Tuesday's 1.8033/38 marks. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's retreat saw sterling/mark give up much of its gains seen after the March minutes of the Bank of England's monetary policy committee meeting showed an even split on keeping UK interest rates steady. Although MPC members were split, the minutes revealed their opinions had become closer. \par \par \uc2  "It suggests the hawks are becoming less hawkish and over time they are becoming convinced there is no need for a rate hike anymore," said Joanne Collins, senior market strategist at Nomura International in London. \par \par \uc2  Sterling/mark was trading at 3.0280/90, down from its post-MPC minutes high of 3.0410 marks and off late Tuesday's level in Europe of 3.0372 marks. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, the pound stood at $1.6840/50 from $1.6844/54 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Andrew Reierson, London newsroom +44 171 542 6745, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4f07a1d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
