\par\par \b \uc2  Japanese Politicians' Rate-Hike Talk Premature, Analysts Say \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nathan Lewis, Staff Reporter \par \uc2 1,056 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 1998\par 15:27\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan's economic slump has become so serious that some senior policy makers in the ruling Liberal Democratic Party are suggesting a contrarian solution: an interest-rate increase. \par \par \uc2  Such talk still has the potential, on occasion, to jolt financial markets. But private analysts widely believe that a rate increase would be a mistake and dismiss the talk as political posturing that has no chance of being implemented in the absence of clear evidence pointing to a Japanese economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "With the deflationary pressure we have now, a rate hike is out of the question," said Tetsufumi Yamakawa, an economist with Goldman Sachs in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Most recently, LDP Deputy Secretary General Hiromu Nonaka suggested in a speech last weekend that the Bank of Japan begin to consider a rate increase. Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto was then quoted by local media as saying he understood Nonaka's sentiments. \par \par \uc2  One argument behind the idea is that miniscule bank-deposit rates are hurting pensioners and others dependent on interest income, curbing consumer spending and sapping consumer confidence. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new governor, Masaru Hayami, acknowledged in late March that ultralow interest rates have had some negative effects. He later said the 0.5% official discount rate, which has remained at that record-low level since September 1995, was "too low, in popular opinion," and that rates could eventually be raised, depending on the progress of an economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  It is true that Japan has ventured beyond normal economic practice in setting its interest rates so low - long-term market rates are the lowest recorded for any economy in nearly 400 years, according to one estimate. So economists concede there are dangers with the current policy. \par \par \uc2  "The demography in Japan is different from other countries, with more elderly, and squeezing interest income probably has more of an effect than it would in, for example, the U.S.," said Larry Duke, an economist with Citibank. \par \par \uc2  With interest rates on savings accounts as low as 0.1%, there is also concern that feeble returns on savings are prompting consumers to save a larger proportion of their income. The consumer-savings rate has risen from about 24% in 1990 to around 28% in 1997 and over 31% in recent months, choking off consumer spending and pushing the economy toward recession. \par \par \uc2  Negligible interest returns in Japan could also prompt greater flows of funds overseas, frustrating authorities' attempts to support the yen. Such flows could be boosted by "Big Bang" financial deregulation, which included the liberalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade this month. Hence, senior LDP figure Seiroku Kajiyama said Wednesday that the Big Bang could result in a Japanese rate hike. \par \par \uc2  Yasushi Okada, a Tokyo-based economist with First Boston, pointed out that there were calls for an interest-rate hike in the U.S. in 1990, when the economy was turning toward recession. \par \par \uc2  But analysts say the weight of economic evidence still argues overwhelmingly against a rate hike. For one thing, the debt-burdened financial system is far too weak to withstand higher interest rates, they say. \par \par \uc2  Salomon Smith Barney economist Jeffrey Young acknowledged that a rise in long-term rates, widening the spread between short and long rates, would help banks. A wide rate spread in the U.S. helped banks rebuild after the bad-debt debacle of the late 1980s. The recent drop in long-term rates in Japan has squeezed banks' profitability and thus their ability to write off bad debts. \par \par \uc2  But higher long-term rates could well cause more corporate bankruptcies, which are already near record levels. And while higher short rates would increase interest returns, they would cause failures among financial institutions that are able to service their debts only because of the current low-rate regime, Young said. \par \par \uc2  In addition, though some consumers would benefit from a rise in rates, it would mean higher costs for others, such as home buyers, Citibank's Duke said. \par \par \uc2  Furthermore, many economists dispute the idea that low interest rates are causing the savings rate to climb. Excluding the two-percentage-point rise in the national sales tax in April 1997, which will disappear from economic data starting this month, the underlying inflation rate is only around 0.1% to 0.2%, with pressure on the downside, Yamakawa of Goldman Sachs estimated. Thus, effective interest rates and savings returns may actually have risen recently, Yamakawa said. \par \par \uc2  "Low interest rates are not a factor" in the rise in the savings rate, Young said. Instead, the rise is because of general fears about the economic future, Duke agreed. \par \par \uc2  And despite the huge interest-rate differential between Japan and overseas, economists believe it is having only a limited effect in encouraging outflows of private capital abroad. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese are very risk-averse right now," Duke said, "and the higher returns overseas aren't enough to justify the exchange-rate risk." \par \par \uc2  First Boston's Okada said that although \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen, it isn't likely to raise interest rates merely on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Last week, in a dramatic loosening of fiscal policy, Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto announced a 16 trillion yen ($120 billion) economic-stimulus package. Duke said a tighter monetary policy, combined with a looser fiscal policy, might be viable at some point, but only after the financial system has become more stable. \par \par \uc2  BOJ governor Hayami has repeatedly made similar comments, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will retain current monetary policy for now and that low interest rates are still having economic benefits. \par \par \uc2  Although Nonaka and other senior LDP officials are influential, they have no formal control over policy, which is up to Hayami and a policy board established this month under a new law designed to make the BOJ more independent of political pressure. \par \par \uc2  The LDP receives much of its support from rural areas that tend to have concentrations of elderly citizens. As the intricacies of monetary policy are beyond most voters, a call for a rate hike amounts to a call for the government to send more money to poorer rural areas, a political tradition with a long history. \par \par \uc2  -By Nathan Lewis; (813) 5255-2929; nlewis@ap.org \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  1139 4206 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du4f00o8e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
