\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Debt Ends Higher; Selling Yen To BOJ Causes Shortage \b0\par\par\uc2 367 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 1998\par 09:08\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Prices of Japanese debt securities\par ended higher Tuesday on continued buying interest as the\par Bank of Japan continued to support its low interest rate\par policy.\par    Senior Bank of Japan officials said late Monday that low\par interest rates are in the best interest of the economy,\par which supported prices this session. The release of the\par minutes from the March 13 policy meeting late this session\par held no surprises for the market, traders said.\par    Although prices rose, trading was actively two-way,\par traders said, and the mid-130 level for the lead contract\par for 10-year bond futures is a comfortable range for the\par market for the near term.\par    Tiffe Dec 3-Mos Euroyen Yield:     0.76%   Dn 0.01\par    SIMEX Dec 3-Mos Euroyen Yield:     0.75%   Dn 0.02\par    TSE June 5-Yr JGB Future Price:    115.11  Up 0.03\par    TSE June 10-Yr JGB Future Price:   130.40  Up 0.17\par    Simex June 10-Yr JGB Future Price: 130.46  Up 0.16\par    10-Yr 3.0% JGB No.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  182 Yield:      1.560%  Dn 0.020\par    The price of the benchmark 10-year bond, the Finance\par Ministry's No. 182, 3.0% coupon due in 2005, ended the Tokyo\par Stock Exchange's morning session at 109.58, up 0.14 from the\par Monday close.\par    Many Japanese banks sold yen to the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  two\par sessions ago when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to support the yen against the dollar.\par    Because the transactions settle today, some market\par participants were having trouble securing yen funding in the\par money market, traders said.\par    The bond market is also eyeing the meeting of finance\par ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven\par industrialized nations to begin in Washington, D.C., on\par Wednesday, although it may not affect bond prices directly.\par    "There will be foreign pressure on Japan to deal with\par economic problems, but we don't expect any big moves in bond\par prices," said a trader at a Japanese securities firm.\par    -By Karen Hawkins; 813-5255-2939, khawkins@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  04-14-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0308EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1037 4153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du4e00ys1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
