\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar softer on mark and yen at midday Europe. \b0\par\par\uc2 576 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 14 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar softened against the yen and the mark by midday in Europe as the market took stock of comments from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) and awaited Wednesday's Group of Seven (G7) meeting. \par \par \uc2  Traders said dollar/yen retreated from overnight highs above 130.35 over fears of further Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Haruhiko Kuroda, head of the MOF's International Finance Bureau, said the G7 shared the view that yen weakness was "excessive". \par \par \uc2  "There is a general sense of wariness ahead of the G7 and Kuroda's comments are just reflecting that," said Tim Fox, chief treasury economist at Standard Chartered Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there was some profit-taking just below the key 130 yen level ahead of Wednesday, when G7 finance ministers and central bankers attending International Monetary Fund (IMF) meetings in Washington will break to discuss the Asia crisis. \par \par \uc2  At 1200 GMT dollar/yen was trading at 129.75/85 from overnight highs around 130.37 and Monday's late North American levels at 129.64/69. \par \par \uc2  They said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears determined to dampen dollar strength after it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly to sell the dollar on Thursday and Friday, knocking dollar/yen to lows near 127.35 yen on Friday from above 133 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "They are really serious about it this time," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  salesman at a major U.S. bank in London, who said he heard the BOJ sold around $4-$6 billion worth of dollars for yen. "It was an issue of restoring credibility for the BOJ. They can't keep talking yen up and have the market keep selling it." \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark slipped off its overnight highs in sympathy with dollar/yen ahead of the Wednesday meetings. \par \par \uc2  There was little impact from weaker-than-expected German wholesale prices for March. The Federal Statistics office reported a 0.3 percent monthly drop compared with a forecast 0.2 percent decline and an unchanged reading in February. \par \par \uc2  At 1030 GMT dollar/mark was trading at 1.8168/78 from overnight highs of 1.8247 and 1.8235/40 in late North American trading on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Economists noted the IMF's World Economic Outlook, released on Monday, showed a rise in economic growth for Germany, but recommended its domestic rates should remain on hold. \par \par \uc2  "The IMF was indicating the mark was a bit undervalued," Standard Chartered's Fox said. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said continental Europe's economy will improve this year, forecasting Germany's growth rate at 2.5 percent in 1998 and 2.8 percent in 1999, up from 2.2 percent in 1997. \par \par \uc2  However, cast against that picture, other economists said the economic picture in the United States was likely to continue to buoy the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. releases its March consumer price index at 1230 GMT on Tuesday. Market economists expect a month-on-month rise of 0.1 percent in headline CPI compared with a rise of 0.1 percent in February. Core CPI, excluding food and energy, is seen up 0.2 percent. March retail sales, also due at 1230 GMT, are expected to show a rise of 0.1 percent from a 0.5 percent rise in February. ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4e07icu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\pi