\par\par \b \uc2  Japan G7 minister fuss highlights insularity. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 1998\par 08:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 14 (Reuters) - Domestic media dubbed it a farce, the main opposition party was up in arms and critics called it Japanese politics at its worst. \par \par \uc2  A tragi-comedy played out over the attendance of Japan's finance minister at a Group of Seven (G7) meeting highlights a political insularity which risks sacrificing global duties on the altar of domestic concerns, analysts said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "At a time when they've already got all kinds of relational problems over their economic policies, to consider not sending the finance minister to a meeting like this for petty domestic tit-for-tat reasons shows you how domestically focused everything is," one foreign economist said. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga said Tuesday morning he hoped to attend a meeting of G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs in Washington, but added that approval was pending. \par \par \uc2  Japan's efforts to rescue its economy from recession and help the rest of Asia recover have been criticised in many quarters as too little and too late, and are expected to be a focus of the G7 meeting. \par \par \uc2  U.S. officials have publicly reserved judgment on the latest planned steps, which include an extra four trillion yen ($30.7 billion) in income tax cuts and hefty public works spending. Washington has also said it shares Tokyo's concern about a weak yen, but has shied away from joining the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  International Monetary Fund (IMF) chief economist Michael Mussa, meanwhile, warned on Monday that Tokyo could see its economy contract despite its latest stimulus plans. \par \par \uc2  One diplomatic source said: "Japan will be the number one issue at the G7 and it's important for Matsunaga to be there and explain his view of the tax cuts and their impact. It would not be received well if the finance minister didn't go." \par \par \uc2  Matsunaga was thrust into limbo after the budget committee of parliament's Upper House scheduled debate on the package of stimulus steps for Wednesday, the same day as the G7 gathering. \par \par \uc2  Japanese parliamentary rules require ministers to obtain permission to absent themselves from such panel sessions. \par \par \uc2  Domestic media said approval would be forthcoming, but by early afternoon on Tuesday officials could still not confirm that Matsunaga would go. \par \par \uc2  Several analysts said the finance minister's absence from the G7 would have little real impact on Japan's presentation of its policies, but the dithering left Matsunaga vulnerable to some pointed quips about his lack of expertise. \par \par \uc2  "I thought they didn't want him to go because he's such a lightweight," the economist said. \par \par \uc2  Matsunaga, a former lawyer with no financial expertise who assumed his post last January, took the heat for Japan's cautious stance on stimulus at a G7 finance meeting in February and will likely face similar pressure this time around. \par \par \uc2  Political sources said the loudest opposition to the trip came from Upper House lawmaker Masakuni Murakami, a ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) member who had locked horns with party executives reluctant to approve extra income tax cuts. \par \par \uc2  Last week Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto decided to put his budget deficit-cutting goal on the back burner to allow the tax cuts to go ahead, but Murakami was still smarting from the earlier rebuff, sources said. \par \par \uc2  "This is LDP politics at its worst," the diplomatic source said. Opposition politicians agreed, insisting in late night talks on the matter than Matsunaga's G7 attendance was vital. \par \par \uc2  A requirement for cabinet ministers to dance in constant attendance when parliament is in session or else seek special dispensation has come under fire before - most recently from outspoken senior Finance Ministry official Eisuke Sakakibara. \par \par \uc2  Visiting cabinet ministers from abroad, meanwhile, have sometimes felt snubbed when their Japanese counterparts handed them over to bureaucrats so they could dash back to parliament. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Environment Minister Hiroshi Oki suffered a major embarrassment last December when he almost had to miss the historic finale of the global warming conference over which he had presided because of parliamentary business. \par \par \uc2  "All politics is domestic, but they have to manage this in a more sensitive manner," the diplomatic source said. \par \par \uc2  Masayoshi Takemura, a former finance minister and a member of the tiny Sakigake party, which is linked with the LDP in a loose ruling alliance, agrees changes must be made. \par \par \uc2  "It may contradict parliamentary practice, but ministers should attend international meetings," Takemura told Reuters on Tuesday. "At the same time, it should not hinder parliamentary debate, so the rules should be revised to allow parliamentary vice ministers to represent ministers in the debates." ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4e07i79\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
