\par\par \b \uc2  Japan MP says MOF minister won't attend G7-Kyodo. \b0\par\par\uc2 241 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 1998\par 10:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 13 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga will not attend the Group of Seven (G7) finance officials' meeting in Washington this week, Kyodo news agency quoted Junzo Iwasaki, the chairman of the Upper House of parliament's budget committee, as saying on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Iwasaki was quoted by Kyodo as saying that Matsunaga would not go to Washington because he had to attend a parliamentary debate on Japan's latest economic stimulus package, which was scheduled for Wednesday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) officials said they had heard it might be difficult for Matsunaga to go to the G7 meeting but could not immediately confirm whether a decision had been made. \par \par \uc2  Japan has been under mounting criticism from its G7 partners over what some see as its inertia in shoring up its sagging economy in order to lead an Asian recovery, and this topic was expected to be on the agenda at this week's meeting. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto last week unveiled an economic stimulus package of over 16 trillion yen, including tax cuts and public works outlays, to push up domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to boost the value of the yen against the U.S. dollar, which Japanese officials say would help capital inflows into Asia from Japan and Asian nations' export competitiveness against Japanese products. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4d07lpj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
