\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's Current-Account Surplus Surges, But Rise Expected To Slow \b0\par\par\uc2 660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 1998\par 03:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan's current-account surplus, a broad measure of trade in goods and services, nearly doubled in February, but the growth is likely to slow in coming months as the data showed the extent to which Asia's economic slowdown is stifling Japanese exports, analysts said Monday. \par \par \uc2  The surplus soared 97.1% from the year-earlier month to 1.665 trillion yen ($12.9 billion), the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. The figure was considerably higher than the average 1.410 trillion yen projected by private economists. \par \par \uc2  But Asia's economic turmoil curtailed growth in Japanese exports to just 2.1%, to 3.981 trillion yen, in February. While the yen has weakened against the dollar, it remains stronger against many Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , detering other Asian economies from buying Japanese goods. \par \par \uc2  Koji Tanami, the top bureaucrat at the finance ministry said that he can't deny Japan's economy is stagnating, and suggested that weak domestic demand helped to boost the current-account surplus. \par \par \uc2  Trends in Japan's current-account statistics generally mirror those of the nation's merchandise-trade balance, which accounts only for the trade in goods, not services and capital income. \par \par \uc2  "Growth in the current-account surplus isn't headed higher, because of the collapse in Asian growth," said Andrew Shipley, chief economist at Schroders Securities (Japan) Ltd. He said the surplus could continue growing at a double-digit rate in March, but not by as much as 90%. \par \par \uc2  The reason for recent growth in the surplus "is not the yen making exports go through the roof, it is due to the dramatic fall in imports. As a result, the economy will continue to shrink, and the yen should fall over the long term," Shipley said. \par \par \uc2  Imports into Japan plunged 17% to 2.509 trillion yen in February, as consumer spending continued to dry up. Exports to Asia comprise about 35% to 40% of Japan's total exports, Shipley said. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said the current-account surplus isn't likely to continue sharply rising. Economists agreed. \par \par \uc2  "Although they've been saying this for some time, this time they have got it right for once," said Matthew Poggi, an economist at Lehman Brothers Japan Inc. "Exports to Asia will continue down. And while exports to the U.S. remain strong, they may slow as the U.S. economy slows, something we see happening in the fall." \par \par \uc2  The ministry official also said a series of economic stimulus steps taken by the government since last year should soon boost domestic demand, increasing imports and helping to curb the current-account surplus. However, analysts were more skeptical of this. \par \par \uc2  "They'd like it to be true, but it's not," Poggi said. "Imports are going to continue to slow quite a bit." \par \par \uc2  With exports and imports falling, the economy is showing further signs of a recession, which is going to put even more pressure on the yen, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently hit a six-and-a-half-year low of 135.42 yen against the dollar before verbal and actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities sent the dollar back below 130 yen. But the weakness of the economy means the Bank of Japan remains unable to raise the official discount rate from its historic low of 0.50%, leaving it with few options to keep the yen from weakening further, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The implications for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are for it to fall to around 140 yen," said Shipley, who believes the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen last week will eventually prove futile. \par \par \uc2  "They're playing with fire," Shipley said of reports that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent between $5 billion and $7 billion protecting the yen. "They can't do that all the time. Foreign reserves can disappear very quickly." \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du4d00n54\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
