\par\par \b \uc2  Japan defends econ steps, markets skeptical. \b0\par\par\uc2  By George Nishiyama \par \uc2 561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 1998\par 02:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Reuters) - Financial markets greeted Japanese Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto's announcement of new tax cuts with a thumbs-down, but officials Friday defended his decision, saying Tokyo had done what was needed. \par \par \uc2  A senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) official told reporters the Japanese economy was unlikely to be a key issue at next week's Group of Seven (G7) meeting in Washington, because Japan has already announced steps to stimulate its economy. \par \par \uc2  "Since Japan has responded to requests from G7 nations (on its economic steps), I don't think there will be any further discussion about Japan's policies at the G7 meeting," the official told a briefing. \par \par \uc2  "We have done whatever is necessary, I think," Japan's top financial diplomat, Eisuke Sakakibara, said earlier. \par \par \uc2  But the markets did not think Tokyo had delivered. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo shares were down more than 1.5 percent at one point Friday, while \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) was required to keep the yen from falling against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had risen above 133 yen Thursday following Hashimoto's announcement, forcing the BOJ to make a rare public \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York. \par \par \uc2  Although the overnight BOJ action briefly pushed the dollar below 130 yen, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was active in the market onFriday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers described BOJ action as "hectic" and "persistent." \par \par \uc2  Officials also gave the yen all the verbal support they could. \par \par \uc2  The senior MOF official said Japan was ready to act in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as needed, and that Tokyo was strongly concerned about an excessive fall in the yen. \par \par \uc2  "We have plenty of ammunition, so we would shoot whenever necessary," he said. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, vice finance minister for international affairs told reporters: "I think the yen will strengthen." \par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the rise of the yen was brought about by aggressive BOJ action and not by a change in the market's view of the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  "The risk of (further) dollar falls has grown, although the long-term outlook for a strong dollar has not changed," a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at a Japanese bank said. \par \par \uc2  As evidence, the yen weakened beyond 130.00 yen at midday, when the BOJ apparently rested its guns for a lunch break. \par \par \uc2  Underscoring deep-rooted pessimism, the government downgraded its official assessment of the economy. \par \par \uc2  "Consumer and corporate sentiment, which worsened last year, are affecting all of the real economy. The economy is stagnant and conditions are becoming more severe," the Economic Planning Agency (EPA) said in its monthly report. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said a lack of detail in Hashimoto's Thursday announcement hurt sentiment in the stock market. \par \par \uc2  A recovery in the benchmark Nikkei average from its earlier lows was due largely to a report saying Hashimoto had hinted at the possibility of permanent tax cuts, they said. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei closed down 0.34 percent at 16,481.12. \par \par \uc2  The financial daily Nihon Keizai Shimbun said Hashimoto had said he might ask the government and ruling Liberal Democratic Party to discuss making a promised income tax cut permanent. \par \par \uc2  The prime minister said in a televised speech Thursday the government would undertake direct fiscal stimulus worth 10 trillion yen, including almost half of that in income tax cuts over two years, to try to get Japan's economy back on track. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4a07k7a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
