\par\par \b \uc2  Japan MOF Kuroda says expects further yen rise. \b0\par\par\uc2 169 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 10 (Reuters) - Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Haruhiko Kuroda said he expects the yen to rise further after jumping against the dollar on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda told Reuters that MOF was ready to take action in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the yen, as it did overnight, but he declined comment on whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on Friday, as traders and bankers believed. \par \par \uc2  Despite the yen's gains against the dollar on Friday, Kuroda said: "I expect the yen to rise further. Don't you?" \par \par \uc2  Asked if the MOF remained prepared for more of what it calls "decisive action" in the market, he said: "We're ready anytime." \par \par \uc2  But he declined to comment on any actions taken during the day. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers estimated the BOJ sold about $5 billion on Friday, knocking the dollar down four yen at one point. ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4a07jmd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
