\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo Stocks Slip 0.3% As Investors Disappointed With Stimulus Plan \b0\par\par\uc2 804 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 1998\par 08:27\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Tokyo share prices closed moderately lower Friday amid disappointment with economic-stimulus steps announced Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar plunged about four yen at one point in Asian trading following \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japan's Nikkei 225 index fell 55.54 points, or 0.3%, to close at 16481.12. The blue-chip index had gained about 1019 points during the previous four sessions. \par \par \uc2  "The economic steps are still mediocre," said Sachio Ishikawa, general manager of the stock division at Chuo Securities. \par \par \uc2  Share prices had been considerably lower earlier in day, but staged a partial recovery from early lows during afternoon trading. \par \par \uc2  Traders took profits after Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto's announcement Thursday of additional tax cuts exceeding 4 trillion yen ($30.48 billion) was well within market expectations. The move is aimed at jump-starting Japan's stalled economy, which is teetering on the verge of recession and has proved unable to regain its lost 1980s vigor. \par \par \uc2  Instead, investors focused on the fact that Hashimoto didn't elaborate on such policy measures as a corporate-tax reduction, the content of public-works spending, and other details of the measures within the package. The head of Japan's Economic Planning Agency, Koji Omi, said Friday morning that full details of the package are expected to be released by the week of April 20. \par \par \uc2  Investors were also disappointed that although Hashimoto said the fiscal-reform law should be reviewed, he added that any changes to allow more fiscal stimulus should be limited. The fiscal-reform law sets goals for reducing Japan's fiscal deficit. A revision would pave the way for further fiscal spending. \par \par \uc2  Market sentiment also was hurt by a report from Japan's Economic Planning Ageny. The economy, stagnant because of weak household spending, falling corporate earnings and flagging capital investment, has showed signs of increasing severity recently, the EPA said in its monthly report. The agency downgraded its assessment of the economy by adding the words "increasingly severe" to describe the stagnant economy. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, massive, repeated and well-conducted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent the dollar plunging against the yen in Asia. \par \par \uc2  In late Asian trading, the dollar was quoted at 128.03 yen, down from 131.20 yen late Thursday in New York. It also was quoted at 1.8168 marks, lower than 1.8235 marks late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had hit an intraday low of 127.42 yen, its lowest level since March 11, when it hit 127.09 yen. Before the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  started its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it was at an intraday high of 131.40 yen. \par \par \uc2  Thin trading conditions ahead of the Easter weekend overseas contributed to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's volatility, traders said. \par \par \uc2  A Japanese monetary official told Dow Jones on condition of anonymity that the Bank of Japan sold "several billion dollars." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action came on the heels of its announced \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday through the Federal Reserve Bank of New York. Although it spent more than $1 billion then to support the yen, the dollar was higher against the yen in early Asian trading. But that quickly changed as Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed it is determined to correct what Japanese authorities have repeatedly said is an "excessively weak" yen. \par \par \uc2  One of the biggest declining sectors was banking, down 1.4%. Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi fell 40 yen to 1,660 yen, Fuji Bank dropped 20 yen to 805 yen and Sanwa Bank slipped 20 yen to 1,180 yen. \par \par \uc2  The banking sector was weighed down by a news-service report that Standard & Poor's estimates the amount of Japanese banks' problem loans at 100 trillion yen. However, the 100-trillion-yen estimate was something S&P had already announced in December, and an S&P official later said they don't have a new estimate on the amount of problem loans. \par \par \uc2  Mitsubishi Chemical ended 4 yen higher at 233 yen on news it will purchase from Hoechst AG interests in three joint polyester-film ventures. \par \par \uc2  Printing company Toppan Forms closed at a record-high of 1,590 yen, up 20 yen, as investors were attracted to the issue for speculative reasons. \par \par \uc2  Taiheiyo Securities dropped 1 yen to 79 yen. The medium-sized Japanese brokerage house said Friday it will close down six domestic branches over the next two months in an effort to further restructure its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of Japan's "Big Bang" financial-market liberalization. \par \par \uc2  Many electronics stocks rose despite the dollar's fall. Sony closed 100 yen higher at 11,400 yen and Sharp added 16 yen to 990 yen. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du4a00muu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
